WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:14.010
PAUL STARES: OK, let's -- is this on?

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:21.720
Yeah, let's get started.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:29.550
Welcome, everybody, to this special roundtable
on "Saudi Arabia in the New Middle East."

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:30.910
Thank you all for coming here.

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:34.640
I'm sure many of you would prefer to be in
Davos at the moment, but we will try to do

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:37.840
our best and compensate for that.

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:40.650
The focus today is obviously Saudi Arabia.

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:46.110
And I think it's fair to say that there are
few countries more important to the global

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:48.520
economy than Saudi Arabia.

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:53.850
And I think it's also fair to say that there
are few countries have been a more steadfast

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:59.110
ally to the United States in the Middle East
for the last six decades.

00:00:59.110 --> 00:01:04.390
But for all of that importance, I think it's
quite remarkable, even shocking, how little

00:01:04.390 --> 00:01:08.000
we still know about the kingdom.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:15.150
There are very few true experts on Saudi Arabia
in the United States, and we're very pleased

00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:21.230
to have two of them here today to talk about
Saudi Arabia.

00:01:21.230 --> 00:01:27.370
We're going to be focusing on several, obviously,
I think, relevant -- currently relevant issues

00:01:27.370 --> 00:01:33.450
relating to Saudi Arabia; firstly, the -- you
know, what's been happening inside the kingdom

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:41.100
since the onset of the Arab upheavals; how
this has been received in the kingdom, how

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:47.100
they've been adapting, what their new posture
in the Middle East has been since the uprisings

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:52.840
began; and then finally what this all bodes
for the future of the U.S. relationship with

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:54.730
the kingdom.

00:01:54.730 --> 00:01:57.370
We will begin first with Gregory Gause.

00:01:57.370 --> 00:02:01.259
He is a professor political science of the
University of Vermont.

00:02:01.259 --> 00:02:07.659
He has held multiple positions before joining
the faculty there.

00:02:07.659 --> 00:02:13.000
He is the author of three books, most recently,
"The International Relations of the Persian

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:20.430
Gulf," and, I should say, most importantly,
this most -- this council special report that's

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:23.200
just come out on "Saudi Arabia in the New
Middle East."

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:26.700
And there should be rare unsigned copies on
your seats.

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:30.390
That's for you to take home with you.

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:35.940
He will talk for about 20 minutes or so, 15,
20 minutes, and then will be followed by Toby

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:40.040
Jones, who is at the History Department at
Rutgers University.

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:47.180
Toby has also held several academic positions
before joining Rutgers and from 2004 to 2006

00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:50.700
was with the International Crisis Group in
the Persian Gulf.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:56.690
He, too, is the author of a book focusing
on Saudi Arabia, "Desert Kingdom: How Oil

00:02:56.690 --> 00:03:00.760
and Water Forged Modern Saudi Arabia."

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:02.510
So I think we will begin.

00:03:02.510 --> 00:03:06.520
I should just say -- mention the ground rules.

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:13.019
The discussion today is on the record, unlike
usual council policy.

00:03:13.019 --> 00:03:23.680
And I also ask you to turn off any cell phones,
other devices, defibrillators excepted, and

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:24.680
we will begin.

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:26.440
So Greg, take it away.

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:29.090
&gt;&gt;F. GREGORY GAUSE: Thanks very much, Paul.

00:03:29.090 --> 00:03:30.840
Am I on here?

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:31.950
&gt;&gt;STARES: Yeah.

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:33.600
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: All right.

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:35.129
Thanks everybody for coming.

00:03:35.129 --> 00:03:39.420
If you're disappointed that you didn't get
invited to Davos, come on up to Vermont; we've

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:44.920
got mountains, we've got a little bit of snow
this season -- not as much as usual, but we

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:49.239
can recreate the Davos experience for you,
and you can get some maple syrup while you're

00:03:49.239 --> 00:03:50.890
there.

00:03:50.890 --> 00:03:51.971
It's a pleasure to be here.

00:03:51.971 --> 00:03:56.090
I want to thank Paul and the council for allowing
me to write this.

00:03:56.090 --> 00:04:01.409
I think I stretch Paul's patience on numerous
occasions.

00:04:01.409 --> 00:04:07.500
He runs the Center for Preventive Action,
and I kind of am an advocate of what I would

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:11.830
call "prudent nonaction" in the Middle East.

00:04:11.830 --> 00:04:17.199
And so he was very tolerant of me on this.

00:04:17.199 --> 00:04:22.669
Let me just say a couple of words about these
three topics that Paul introduced.

00:04:22.669 --> 00:04:26.770
One is the internal dynamics within the country.

00:04:26.770 --> 00:04:32.720
My bottom line is that I think this is a very
stable country in terms of regime stability,

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:35.439
the political stability of the regime.

00:04:35.439 --> 00:04:40.710
We have, I think, in the United States a tendency
when we see upheavals in the Middle East to

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:44.180
automatically assume that Saudi Arabia is
going to be next.

00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:48.759
I think that's largely because they're a monarchy,
and we don't understand or appreciate monarchies,

00:04:48.759 --> 00:04:52.000
having gotten rid of one ourselves some years
ago.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:05:00.279
Sorry about that, Paul, but -- (laughter)
-- and because, I think, they wear funny clothes.

00:05:00.279 --> 00:05:04.550
And so we just think that they must be on
the verge.

00:05:04.550 --> 00:05:10.439
But I think the regime is quite remarkable
in how it has weathered the various storms

00:05:10.439 --> 00:05:13.089
that it has faced in the modern Middle East.

00:05:13.089 --> 00:05:14.289
So how does it do it?

00:05:14.289 --> 00:05:16.210
Particularly, how did it do it this time?

00:05:16.210 --> 00:05:20.210
I think that there are three important things
to highlight, and the most important and obvious

00:05:20.210 --> 00:05:21.899
one is money.

00:05:21.899 --> 00:05:23.139
Right?

00:05:23.139 --> 00:05:31.120
Not a single oil regime fell of its own accord
in the Arab Spring.

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:32.120
All right?

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:35.740
Libya had -- required Western military intervention
to remove that regime.

00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:43.180
No other oil regime fell in the Arab Spring,
and very few oil regimes even faced serious

00:05:43.180 --> 00:05:48.949
upheaval, Libya being the most notable exception,
which just goes to show that the money doesn't

00:05:48.949 --> 00:05:49.949
spend itself.

00:05:49.949 --> 00:05:50.949
Right?

00:05:50.949 --> 00:05:54.210
And if you have an idiot spending the money
or not spending the money, the money doesn't

00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:55.899
do any good.

00:05:55.899 --> 00:05:57.110
Right?

00:05:57.110 --> 00:05:58.389
But it's not just monarchies.

00:05:58.389 --> 00:05:59.389
Right?

00:05:59.389 --> 00:06:00.669
It's Algeria, Iraq.

00:06:00.669 --> 00:06:05.449
These places did not have the same kinds of
problems that many of their neighbors had.

00:06:05.449 --> 00:06:10.139
So with oil at a hundred -- over a hundred
dollars a barrel, that gives regimes quite

00:06:10.139 --> 00:06:15.460
a few carrots and sticks to deal with restive
populations.

00:06:15.460 --> 00:06:20.020
The second reason I think that the Saudis
weathered this is their networks, and money

00:06:20.020 --> 00:06:26.099
is part of this, but the political skill of
maintaining networks is something that is

00:06:26.099 --> 00:06:28.309
the best of the al-Saud family.

00:06:28.309 --> 00:06:30.169
They portion this out.

00:06:30.169 --> 00:06:35.909
They have portfolios for various princes to
deal with various groups.

00:06:35.909 --> 00:06:37.969
They have networks with the business community.

00:06:37.969 --> 00:06:42.349
They have networks with the -- with the tribes,
which are socially important but politically,

00:06:42.349 --> 00:06:48.219
I would argue, almost completely emasculated.

00:06:48.219 --> 00:06:52.650
And that has been -- a project of the al-Saud
family is to keep -- is to -- is to destroy

00:06:52.650 --> 00:06:55.319
tribal political power.

00:06:55.319 --> 00:07:02.240
With Shi'a leaders, with regional leaders
in the Hejaz, with intellectuals, with every

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:08.199
group that you can imagine, sports clubs,
the al-Saud family has its networks here,

00:07:08.199 --> 00:07:11.459
and it activated all of those networks during
this year.

00:07:11.459 --> 00:07:16.429
And finally, I think that one of the most
important things that distinguishes cases

00:07:16.429 --> 00:07:21.459
in the Arab world where the regime feel from
cases where the regime is still holding on

00:07:21.459 --> 00:07:26.569
or didn't suffer any crisis at all is the
ability of the opposition to overcome its

00:07:26.569 --> 00:07:37.280
own internal ideological and social, sectarian,
ethnic -- whatever you want to call them -- social

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:44.330
differences, and unite behind a common -- a
common goal of getting rid of the regime.

00:07:44.330 --> 00:07:48.279
In Saudi Arabia, the opposition has never
been able to do that, and the opposition is

00:07:48.279 --> 00:07:52.989
divided on sectarian lines; it is divided
on ideological lines.

00:07:52.989 --> 00:08:00.789
Even during the upheavals of earlier this
year, those who were looking for political

00:08:00.789 --> 00:08:04.790
change in Saudi Arabia had to put together
two major petitions, not one, because they

00:08:04.790 --> 00:08:06.270
couldn't agree to get together on one petition.

00:08:06.270 --> 00:08:10.339
If you can't agree to get together on one
petition, you're certainly not going to overthrow

00:08:10.339 --> 00:08:12.690
a regime, it seems to me.

00:08:12.690 --> 00:08:15.060
That doesn't mean that the Saudis are out
of the woods.

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:17.050
They do have some medium-term issues.

00:08:17.050 --> 00:08:23.509
Obviously, the succession issue is one that
we as outsiders have very, very little insight

00:08:23.509 --> 00:08:28.849
into, but it is a serious problem for the
regime.

00:08:28.849 --> 00:08:31.469
And I don't mean from King Abdullah to Prince
Nayef.

00:08:31.469 --> 00:08:38.310
I mean from this current generation of rulers
to the next generation, to the grandsons of

00:08:38.310 --> 00:08:41.180
the founding kind of the third kingdom.

00:08:41.180 --> 00:08:46.240
That is a -- that is a transition that has
no precedent and will be a very interesting

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:47.240
thing to watch.

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:50.149
But it's not going to happen this year or
next.

00:08:50.149 --> 00:08:52.649
Fiscal issues -- all right?

00:08:52.649 --> 00:08:57.550
Just the other day, Ali Naimi said that a
hundred dollars a barrel is what the Saudis'

00:08:57.550 --> 00:08:59.410
price goal is.

00:08:59.410 --> 00:09:00.779
Toby and I were talking before lunch.

00:09:00.779 --> 00:09:02.649
We can -- we're not that old.

00:09:02.649 --> 00:09:03.649
He's even younger than I am.

00:09:03.649 --> 00:09:09.120
But we can remember when the price point was
$28 a barrel, and it wasn't that long ago.

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:10.120
All right?

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:15.720
A hundred dollars a barrel -- that means that
the Saudis need that money -- paying these

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:23.500
networks to maintain the system, and thus,
I think that -- although I'm no -- I wouldn't

00:09:23.500 --> 00:09:28.310
even claim that I could protect the oil markets;
I would -- I would not be a penurious academic

00:09:28.310 --> 00:09:33.120
if I could predict the oil markets -- we don't
know if oil's going to be a hundred dollars

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:36.720
a barrel or $110 for the next five or six
years.

00:09:36.720 --> 00:09:37.720
Who knows?

00:09:37.720 --> 00:09:43.110
A fiscal squeeze, if oil goes down below $80
a barrel or $70 a barrel, would be a very

00:09:43.110 --> 00:09:45.100
interesting challenge for the Saudis.

00:09:45.100 --> 00:09:51.620
Finally, I think that -- one of the things
-- to me the most interesting thing that's

00:09:51.620 --> 00:09:59.959
happened this year, this past year, in the
Middle East is I think that -- I mean, Fukuyama's

00:09:59.959 --> 00:10:02.220
"End of History" finally came to the Middle
East.

00:10:02.220 --> 00:10:03.220
Right?

00:10:03.220 --> 00:10:08.459
And I think just at a time when Fukuyama has
backed away from "End of History," I actually

00:10:08.459 --> 00:10:11.029
think the thesis is more powerful than ever.

00:10:11.029 --> 00:10:12.029
Right?

00:10:12.029 --> 00:10:18.300
I think that the events in the Middle East
this past year have basically said that there

00:10:18.300 --> 00:10:24.519
is no defensible political system that doesn't
have a democratic element to it.

00:10:24.519 --> 00:10:26.920
That doesn't mean that we're going to have
democracy and that doesn't mean people aren't

00:10:26.920 --> 00:10:29.649
going to push against it.

00:10:29.649 --> 00:10:35.689
And one of the reasons I say this is because
of the reaction of Salafi Islamists to this

00:10:35.689 --> 00:10:37.940
-- to the events of the past year.

00:10:37.940 --> 00:10:38.940
Right?

00:10:38.940 --> 00:10:43.139
I'm no fan of the Salafis, even though I spent
some time in Riyadh.

00:10:43.139 --> 00:10:50.670
And I was troubled by their -- what a significant
portion of the vote they got in Egypt.

00:10:50.670 --> 00:10:56.639
But if one steps back and says, this was the
element of Islamist politics that had been

00:10:56.639 --> 00:11:04.129
explicitly anti-democratic, that had said
on doctrinal grounds that democracy is rejected;

00:11:04.129 --> 00:11:09.420
it's an innovation; it's un-Islamic; we don't
need it -- and now not only do we see Salafis

00:11:09.420 --> 00:11:16.060
participating in the electoral process in
Egypt, but we see Salafi figures in Saudi

00:11:16.060 --> 00:11:20.699
Arabia signing petitions calling for an elected
national legislature.

00:11:20.699 --> 00:11:27.850
We see Salafi figures in Saudi Arabia publicly
praising the Egyptian revolution and supporting

00:11:27.850 --> 00:11:28.850
it.

00:11:28.850 --> 00:11:32.529
This is something that I think in the -- in
the longer term will have to trouble the Saudi

00:11:32.529 --> 00:11:40.459
regime, because it has always based its right
to rule on the idea that it represents the

00:11:40.459 --> 00:11:45.350
true Islam, right, this Salafist strain of
Islam.

00:11:45.350 --> 00:11:52.310
And in the end, it seems to me that coming
out of 2011, one of the important turns is

00:11:52.310 --> 00:11:58.839
that the only people who now argue that there
are bases other than some form of popular

00:11:58.839 --> 00:12:07.620
consent upon which to base regimes are the
official clergy in Saudi Arabia, the proponents

00:12:07.620 --> 00:12:12.079
of Velayat-e faqih in Iran, and al Qaeda.

00:12:12.079 --> 00:12:15.190
And that doesn't seem to be a winning team
in the future.

00:12:15.190 --> 00:12:19.290
I'm not predicting waves of democracy in the
future.

00:12:19.290 --> 00:12:20.290
I see &gt;&gt;MR.

00:12:20.290 --> 00:12:21.290
Abrams in the back.

00:12:21.290 --> 00:12:23.370
I'm still not on the team.

00:12:23.370 --> 00:12:30.910
But I do think that -- I do think that this
has been a major shift that will have long-term

00:12:30.910 --> 00:12:32.710
consequences for Saudi Arabia and the region.

00:12:32.710 --> 00:12:38.160
OK, regionally, briefly, I don't see Saudi
Arabia as counterrevolutionary.

00:12:38.160 --> 00:12:40.071
I think that that's too easy a trope.

00:12:40.071 --> 00:12:43.029
And my friend Toby Jones will tell me why
I'm wrong in a minute, but let me set out

00:12:43.029 --> 00:12:44.360
why.

00:12:44.360 --> 00:12:48.610
I think that Saudi Arabia's clearly counterrevolutionary
when it comes to monarchy.

00:12:48.610 --> 00:12:51.010
It sees monarchy as absolutely essential.

00:12:51.010 --> 00:12:56.010
It sees the preservation of monarchy, particularly
in its geographical area, the Gulf -- and

00:12:56.010 --> 00:13:01.720
we'll include Jordan in that -- as absolutely
essential to its own domestic regime security

00:13:01.720 --> 00:13:02.949
and stability.

00:13:02.949 --> 00:13:07.420
And thus for Saudi Arabia, the preservation
of monarchy in the Gulf Cooperation Council

00:13:07.420 --> 00:13:13.110
and in Jordan is a domestic political imperative.

00:13:13.110 --> 00:13:19.420
However, once we get beyond that, it seems
to that what Saudi Arabia -- the lens through

00:13:19.420 --> 00:13:26.540
which Saudi Arabia is looking at the Arab
-- the events of 2011 is through its regional

00:13:26.540 --> 00:13:28.490
competition with Iran.

00:13:28.490 --> 00:13:33.879
And thus it's more than willing to support
regime change in a place like Syria.

00:13:33.879 --> 00:13:39.199
It was willing to support regime change, obviously,
in Libya -- but that was, I think, more because

00:13:39.199 --> 00:13:45.680
of the distinctive characteristics of the
brother leader and his past history with Saudi

00:13:45.680 --> 00:13:51.720
Arabia -- and was willing, however reluctantly
and however cautiously and non-democratically

00:13:51.720 --> 00:13:55.920
-- very happy to admit that -- to ease or
try to ease Ali Abdullah Saleh out of power

00:13:55.920 --> 00:13:58.329
in Yemen.

00:13:58.329 --> 00:14:01.889
So to me, that's not counterrevolutionary.

00:14:01.889 --> 00:14:07.990
That's a country that's confused, defensive
and basically looking through -- looking at

00:14:07.990 --> 00:14:13.600
the region through the prism of a political
competition with Iran.

00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:19.559
Finally, the American agenda -- I stole the
word "transactional," which if you looked

00:14:19.559 --> 00:14:26.310
at the pamphlet, is the kind of keyword for
the -- my recommendations for the U.S.-Saudi

00:14:26.310 --> 00:14:27.310
relationship.

00:14:27.310 --> 00:14:35.129
I stole that from the irrepressible Chas Freeman,
who was the chairman of our advisory group.

00:14:35.129 --> 00:14:39.490
But I do think it captures something important
in that the relationship with Saudi Arabia

00:14:39.490 --> 00:14:44.680
is -- has been going through a slow-motion
change since the end of the Cold War, really

00:14:44.680 --> 00:14:49.540
since the 1970s, I would say, when the Saudis
in effect took over their old -- their own

00:14:49.540 --> 00:14:50.680
oil industry.

00:14:50.680 --> 00:14:57.029
The two great pillars historically of American-Saudi
cooperation were Saudi operation of -- I mean,

00:14:57.029 --> 00:15:03.210
American operation of the Saudi oil industry
and a common Cold War understanding of the

00:15:03.210 --> 00:15:04.569
globe.

00:15:04.569 --> 00:15:08.980
And that doesn't mean we agreed on everything
-- oil embargo in 1973.

00:15:08.980 --> 00:15:09.980
Right?

00:15:09.980 --> 00:15:13.629
But those two pillars are no longer.

00:15:13.629 --> 00:15:20.509
We have numerous common interests geopolitically
in the region, but I don't think that we can

00:15:20.509 --> 00:15:24.649
assume that the Saudis are going to be with
us on everything the way they were in the

00:15:24.649 --> 00:15:29.640
'80s, when we were partners in all sorts of
anti-Soviet activities.

00:15:29.640 --> 00:15:32.920
I don't think that the Saudis should assume
that we'll always be with them.

00:15:32.920 --> 00:15:36.689
I think we have to take it issue by issue.

00:15:36.689 --> 00:15:41.870
I think that one of the dynamics that is becoming
increasingly apparent is that Saudi Arabia

00:15:41.870 --> 00:15:45.449
is no longer an oil moderate; it's no longer
a dove in the oil market.

00:15:45.449 --> 00:15:50.380
It doesn't play the role that it seemed to
-- that it played in the '80s and '90s.

00:15:50.380 --> 00:15:52.680
It's always been more complicated than that.

00:15:52.680 --> 00:15:59.730
But when Ali Naimi, the oil minister, says
the other day that the target price for Saudi

00:15:59.730 --> 00:16:05.610
Arabia for oil is a hundred dollars a barrel,
that's not what I would call a price moderate.

00:16:05.610 --> 00:16:11.180
So I think that on those issues, the assumption
-- as we're entering an election year, American

00:16:11.180 --> 00:16:14.410
presidents like to call Saudi kings during
election years and say, hey, do something

00:16:14.410 --> 00:16:15.920
about those oil prices.

00:16:15.920 --> 00:16:20.930
I'm not sure that that kind of thing is going
to fly anymore.

00:16:20.930 --> 00:16:26.029
Two issues that I think are extremely important
in which we have common interests but also

00:16:26.029 --> 00:16:30.499
different viewpoints that are going to require
a lot of conversation with the Saudis: One

00:16:30.499 --> 00:16:35.490
is the rise of sectarianism in the politics
of the Arab East.

00:16:35.490 --> 00:16:36.490
This is Bahrain.

00:16:36.490 --> 00:16:37.490
This is Iraq.

00:16:37.490 --> 00:16:38.770
This is Syria.

00:16:38.770 --> 00:16:47.519
This is -- North Africa, thank God, is immune
to this because of its own demographic profile,

00:16:47.519 --> 00:16:51.010
but the Arab East is riven with sectarianism
now.

00:16:51.010 --> 00:16:55.370
Sectarianism is not a new thing, but its salience
rises and falls over time, and it's extremely

00:16:55.370 --> 00:16:56.370
high right now.

00:16:56.370 --> 00:16:58.250
And Saudi Arabia is pushing it.

00:16:58.250 --> 00:17:02.200
One of the reasons that sectarianism is so
salient now is Saudi Arabia's chosen to play

00:17:02.200 --> 00:17:03.200
that card.

00:17:03.200 --> 00:17:06.789
I think that that's very dangerous for American
interests.

00:17:06.789 --> 00:17:13.689
Sectarian -- this atmosphere of sectarian
hospitality is a breeding ground for Sunni

00:17:13.689 --> 00:17:17.130
radicalism of the al Qaeda variety.

00:17:17.130 --> 00:17:21.990
I also think that it's very negative in terms
of the future of Iraq.

00:17:21.990 --> 00:17:25.990
I think that if there's a practical policy
issue here -- I think getting the Saudis to

00:17:25.990 --> 00:17:31.840
engage with the Iraqi government, as distasteful
as they find that government, is an imperative.

00:17:31.840 --> 00:17:36.072
And I also think it's self-defeating because,
you know, frankly, you push -- you know, Arab

00:17:36.072 --> 00:17:42.809
Sh'ia are not particularly important, except
in the Gulf region and Lebanon.

00:17:42.809 --> 00:17:44.299
Right?

00:17:44.299 --> 00:17:49.910
And to the extent that sectarianism dominates
politics, that drives them toward Iran.

00:17:49.910 --> 00:17:56.230
Framing things in an Arab-versus-Persian context
can actually help bring these folks in.

00:17:56.230 --> 00:17:59.080
And I know that there are people in Saudi
Arabia who are troubled by this, too, and

00:17:59.080 --> 00:18:01.409
see this as a difficult problem.

00:18:01.409 --> 00:18:06.070
And finally, the nuclear question: I think
that there are very strong incentives among

00:18:06.070 --> 00:18:10.660
Saudi leaders that if Iran were to cross the
nuclear threshold, however we define that

00:18:10.660 --> 00:18:17.669
crossing, then there would be enormous support
within the Saudi elite for trying to obtain

00:18:17.669 --> 00:18:19.960
their own nuclear weapons.

00:18:19.960 --> 00:18:21.350
I don't think that's inevitable.

00:18:21.350 --> 00:18:24.800
I think a lot depends on their relationship
with us, but I think that that's a conversation

00:18:24.800 --> 00:18:30.110
we have to start having with them, because
I think that if that happens, if they come

00:18:30.110 --> 00:18:34.370
to an understanding that Iran is now a nuclear
power, however they define that -- and I understand

00:18:34.370 --> 00:18:39.110
that that's a complicated question -- then
things could move very quickly and uncharacteristically

00:18:39.110 --> 00:18:40.110
for the Saudis.

00:18:40.110 --> 00:18:44.560
I think things could move very quickly on
their side, and I think that that's a conversation

00:18:44.560 --> 00:18:46.309
that's worth having with them right now.

00:18:46.309 --> 00:18:47.309
Thanks, Paul.

00:18:47.309 --> 00:18:48.309
&gt;&gt;STARES: Great.

00:18:48.309 --> 00:18:49.840
Thanks very much, Greg.

00:18:49.840 --> 00:18:50.840
Toby?

00:18:50.840 --> 00:18:55.520
&gt;&gt;TOBY JONES: Thanks, also, to Paul and the
council for bringing me down.

00:18:55.520 --> 00:19:01.600
I'm an unenviable position, because I'm pressed
to respond or charged with the responsibility

00:19:01.600 --> 00:19:06.320
of responding with somebody on key points
that I agree with.

00:19:06.320 --> 00:19:12.070
So it's sort of hard, in one sense, to kind
of point to what I think are critical weaknesses

00:19:12.070 --> 00:19:18.279
or points in divergence in Greg's -- you know,
this excellent report.

00:19:18.279 --> 00:19:23.669
But I do think there are places where there
are opportunities for at least considering

00:19:23.669 --> 00:19:27.330
if not more nuance at least more uncertainty.

00:19:27.330 --> 00:19:32.030
Where I'm not -- it's not quite as clear to
me that things will move in a direction or

00:19:32.030 --> 00:19:37.360
have moved in a direction that necessarily
point to either stability or the indefinite

00:19:37.360 --> 00:19:40.750
necessity of maintaining a strong relationship
-- for the Americans to maintain a strong

00:19:40.750 --> 00:19:41.970
relationship with Saudi Arabia.

00:19:41.970 --> 00:19:47.990
So I'm going to speak on two sets of issues,
both of which Greg raised in his report and

00:19:47.990 --> 00:19:49.330
that he spoke about today.

00:19:49.330 --> 00:19:56.260
And I'm going to, on the first one, suggest
an alternative viewpoint on Saudi Arabia's

00:19:56.260 --> 00:20:01.800
stability and where it may or may not go in
the short to medium term.

00:20:01.800 --> 00:20:07.779
I'll offer this as a sort of preliminary comment
for the kind of analytical view that I'll

00:20:07.779 --> 00:20:10.009
offer up in just a few moments.

00:20:10.009 --> 00:20:13.870
Past stability is no predictability of future
stability.

00:20:13.870 --> 00:20:16.730
I'm a historian and so I don't predict the
future.

00:20:16.730 --> 00:20:22.059
But boy, if the Arab Spring taught historians
anything, it's that autocratic Arab regimes

00:20:22.059 --> 00:20:28.679
are not stable and that long-standing claims
that these were durable political systems

00:20:28.679 --> 00:20:30.690
surely hasn't weathered 2011.

00:20:30.690 --> 00:20:35.840
And we have to think about the various possibilities
for places like Saudi Arabia, oil producers

00:20:35.840 --> 00:20:41.549
and others, who have yet to experience the
kind of political mobilization and mass uprising

00:20:41.549 --> 00:20:46.799
and ask the question, are they in fact vulnerable
to the sorts of shocks that brought down regimes

00:20:46.799 --> 00:20:51.940
elsewhere, whether it's a combination of domestic
or external forces that bring them down.

00:20:51.940 --> 00:20:55.700
The second issue and set of questions that
-- second set of issues that I want to touch

00:20:55.700 --> 00:21:02.789
on is, I want to sort of encourage a more
critical and perhaps revisionist view of the

00:21:02.789 --> 00:21:08.309
U.S.-Saudi relationship not necessarily historically
but grounded in more recent events, and the

00:21:08.309 --> 00:21:12.510
role that Saudi Arabia -- and to sort of encourage
thinking about the role that Saudi Arabia

00:21:12.510 --> 00:21:16.830
actually plays in the Middle East, and the
way that the United States has talked about

00:21:16.830 --> 00:21:19.070
its interests and raised the question.

00:21:19.070 --> 00:21:20.570
Are they actually the same?

00:21:20.570 --> 00:21:23.169
And I don't think they are.

00:21:23.169 --> 00:21:27.330
So on the first set of issues, who no -- which
I think can be boiled down to this question:

00:21:27.330 --> 00:21:31.070
Why was there no Arab Spring in Saudi Arabia?

00:21:31.070 --> 00:21:37.860
Or more directly in response to Greg's report,
was Saudi Arabia in fact little affected by

00:21:37.860 --> 00:21:38.970
the events of 2011?

00:21:38.970 --> 00:21:43.760
It's true that there were no street protests
of any consequence, the regime was not toppled,

00:21:43.760 --> 00:21:48.370
nor was it ever seriously threatened by the
kind of social and political mobilization

00:21:48.370 --> 00:21:51.210
that took place elsewhere.

00:21:51.210 --> 00:21:57.480
But I want to suggest that in fact if you
revisit the events of early 2011, a period

00:21:57.480 --> 00:22:01.630
of great uncertainty in the region, when friendly
regimes were falling from power, Mubarak most

00:22:01.630 --> 00:22:03.500
notably, but not only Mubarak.

00:22:03.500 --> 00:22:08.299
It also was important to recall that the first
indications of uprisings in Yemen, Bahrain

00:22:08.299 --> 00:22:13.720
and Syria also first materialized in February
and early March, that Saudi Arabia was deeply

00:22:13.720 --> 00:22:19.440
anxious and certain about what its own political
future was vis-a-vis its regional neighbors.

00:22:19.440 --> 00:22:21.769
And in fact, it in responded with alarm.

00:22:21.769 --> 00:22:27.750
I had a piece that appeared at Foreign Policy
that made the case that there was a deep sense

00:22:27.750 --> 00:22:32.130
of anxiety that had settled in over Riyadh
and that they were acting with some sense

00:22:32.130 --> 00:22:36.179
of desperation and trying to both preempt
mobilization at home, as well as to figure

00:22:36.179 --> 00:22:38.400
out what was going on in the rest of the region.

00:22:38.400 --> 00:22:42.370
And yes, I was one of the early proponents
of Saudi Arabia as a counterrevolutionary

00:22:42.370 --> 00:22:43.370
force.

00:22:43.370 --> 00:22:44.620
I'll come back to that, thinking regionally.

00:22:44.620 --> 00:22:49.429
But I think just as important is, how did
Saudi Arabia handle its domestic affairs,

00:22:49.429 --> 00:22:53.789
and why was there no mass political mobilization?

00:22:53.789 --> 00:22:59.510
Greg outlined what I think are the key points
in terms of its instrumental approach to dealing

00:22:59.510 --> 00:23:05.110
with domestic crisis and the possibility of
political ferment: money, patronage, the mobilization

00:23:05.110 --> 00:23:11.250
of Islam in important ways, and also the ways
it moved Saudi domestic pieces on the chessboard.

00:23:11.250 --> 00:23:16.110
The al-Saud have historically demonstrated
nothing if they're not masters of dividing

00:23:16.110 --> 00:23:20.350
and pitting various domestic constituencies
against one another.

00:23:20.350 --> 00:23:24.630
But it's also important to note that it was
no guarantee that this was going to work.

00:23:24.630 --> 00:23:30.279
And I'll come back to how I think this actually
played out in important ways -- that in fact

00:23:30.279 --> 00:23:33.330
Saudi Arabia is an incredibly diverse place.

00:23:33.330 --> 00:23:37.460
It's diverse religiously, culturally, politically,
geographically, ethnically -- well, maybe

00:23:37.460 --> 00:23:41.740
not ethnically, but, you know, let's not push
it too far.

00:23:41.740 --> 00:23:47.419
I mean, so -- but you know, this is a large
place, and it's controlled by a group of political

00:23:47.419 --> 00:23:53.679
elites that have often understood that they
are sort of presented with the challenge of

00:23:53.679 --> 00:23:59.730
dealing with and managing a society and a
population that often sees itself at odds

00:23:59.730 --> 00:24:01.700
with their political masters.

00:24:01.700 --> 00:24:05.960
And don't be fooled by the absence of kind
of public declarations of disillusion with

00:24:05.960 --> 00:24:06.960
the Saudi regime.

00:24:06.960 --> 00:24:11.200
There is a deep sense of frustration in Saudi
Arabia with political corruption, with the

00:24:11.200 --> 00:24:16.279
absence of political possibility, with the
nature of autocracy at home and all of the

00:24:16.279 --> 00:24:17.639
excess that it means.

00:24:17.639 --> 00:24:21.920
These have been long-standing concerns amongst
important sectors of the Saudi population.

00:24:21.920 --> 00:24:27.820
From the late 1970s, we've seen episodes of
political dissent at home that, sure, of course,

00:24:27.820 --> 00:24:32.740
it hasn't materialized in a widespread revolutionary
movement, but that nevertheless has been persistent

00:24:32.740 --> 00:24:35.010
over the last three or four decades.

00:24:35.010 --> 00:24:40.519
The reason that Saudi dissidents, not only
the opposition but other political forces,

00:24:40.519 --> 00:24:47.070
have not been able to mobilize or come together
in any meaningful way is not indication of

00:24:47.070 --> 00:24:49.639
the absence of the political will to do so.

00:24:49.639 --> 00:24:51.730
I think it comes down to two things.

00:24:51.730 --> 00:24:55.399
One is that the state has tremendous coercive
power.

00:24:55.399 --> 00:24:58.460
Greg mentioned in the report the power of
its security forces.

00:24:58.460 --> 00:25:03.010
It's important to note that in early March
the Saudis deployed thousands of police forces

00:25:03.010 --> 00:25:05.169
to preempt the day of rage that was called
for.

00:25:05.169 --> 00:25:09.640
If I'm a Saudi and I live in Riyadh -- and
Riyadh doesn't have the kind of public sphere

00:25:09.640 --> 00:25:13.480
that Cairo has -- where am I going to converge?

00:25:13.480 --> 00:25:17.461
Where is that public space where we can make
a united, you know, sort of demand, or call

00:25:17.461 --> 00:25:18.461
on political reform?

00:25:18.461 --> 00:25:19.461
There isn't one.

00:25:19.461 --> 00:25:22.160
I mean, Riyadh is sort of mapped out and laid
out much like Chicago.

00:25:22.160 --> 00:25:24.570
It's a big grid, except for the old city.

00:25:24.570 --> 00:25:28.049
There are no obvious places for people to
mobilize.

00:25:28.049 --> 00:25:32.039
Double that or couple that with the presence
of a large number of security forces.

00:25:32.039 --> 00:25:34.570
Of course there was no day of rage in Saudi
Arabia.

00:25:34.570 --> 00:25:38.470
If Saudis needed any indication of how they
would have been treated, they could have easily

00:25:38.470 --> 00:25:43.220
imagined a Syria-like scenario unfolding or
a Bahrain-like scenario unfolding to keep

00:25:43.220 --> 00:25:44.220
them from doing so.

00:25:44.220 --> 00:25:45.220
Right?

00:25:45.220 --> 00:25:49.060
So there was a powerful security apparatus
and disincentives for people to take to the

00:25:49.060 --> 00:25:53.470
public square, and there was a limited public
square to take to in the first place.

00:25:53.470 --> 00:25:58.230
The other -- and I think this is much more
important in some ways, speaking to both Saudi

00:25:58.230 --> 00:26:02.210
Arabia's political strategy, as well as the
legacy of a certain a kind of politics that

00:26:02.210 --> 00:26:05.889
has gripped the country or that has guided
the country over the last three, four decades,

00:26:05.889 --> 00:26:11.039
and that is the various ways that Saudi Arabia's
leaders have mobilized both Islam and culture

00:26:11.039 --> 00:26:13.800
in order to accomplish short-term political
goals.

00:26:13.800 --> 00:26:19.460
If you think of who the two most restive groups
in Saudi Arabia were in 2011, it was Shi'ites,

00:26:19.460 --> 00:26:25.510
and it was women -- troubled communities,
unlikely revolutionaries and certainly unlikely

00:26:25.510 --> 00:26:30.760
to capture the attention of tens of thousands
or even millions of their fellow citizens.

00:26:30.760 --> 00:26:33.059
Women's issues are polarizing in Saudi Arabia.

00:26:33.059 --> 00:26:37.270
They mobilize people in support of women as
much as they mobilize people in opposition

00:26:37.270 --> 00:26:38.669
to women's rights.

00:26:38.669 --> 00:26:43.481
The question of gender and gender relations
is highly charged and problematic, and the

00:26:43.481 --> 00:26:45.539
Saudi state understands this.

00:26:45.539 --> 00:26:50.850
And when it can put women rather than political
reform or constitutional monarchy or equal

00:26:50.850 --> 00:26:54.970
rights or participation at the center of analysis
in political discourse, it knows it's got

00:26:54.970 --> 00:26:57.220
a winning strategy.

00:26:57.220 --> 00:27:01.570
The same thing is true when it comes to sectarianism
and Shi'ites.

00:27:01.570 --> 00:27:04.780
Greg mentioned that Saudi Arabia is in many
ways problematic in the way that it plays

00:27:04.780 --> 00:27:05.790
the sectarian card.

00:27:05.790 --> 00:27:07.210
I mean, it's important to know.

00:27:07.210 --> 00:27:10.170
Shi'ites are a minority in Saudi Arabia.

00:27:10.170 --> 00:27:12.590
Perhaps they make up 10, 15 percent of the
population.

00:27:12.590 --> 00:27:16.010
That's a pretty significant number of folks
-- a million, a million and a half, maybe

00:27:16.010 --> 00:27:19.010
at the outside 2 million people.

00:27:19.010 --> 00:27:25.780
When they became active in March -- late February,
early March 2011, the regime rushed in very

00:27:25.780 --> 00:27:30.730
quickly to mobilize on this moment and to
use their activism as a way to discredit other

00:27:30.730 --> 00:27:35.320
potential civic or civil disobedience or public
dissent.

00:27:35.320 --> 00:27:40.019
So the state then plays upon both the question
of women and religious difference as a way

00:27:40.019 --> 00:27:41.680
to divide society.

00:27:41.680 --> 00:27:46.790
Greg mentioned sort of the division -- sort
of divide-and-conquer mentality and approach

00:27:46.790 --> 00:27:52.780
to domestic politics that has long characterized
Saudi Arabia's approach to its own citizens.

00:27:52.780 --> 00:27:58.000
This was on fully display earlier this year,
and it's incredibly effective.

00:27:58.000 --> 00:28:02.230
Saudi Arabia has a highly atomized society
in which people don't necessarily identify

00:28:02.230 --> 00:28:07.240
with one another across gender or class or
sectarian lines, religious lines.

00:28:07.240 --> 00:28:13.970
And so I think to a large extent, the state's
ability to play upon these, manipulate them

00:28:13.970 --> 00:28:19.399
and fan the flames of uncertainty about Shi'ism,
as well as to use women as a wedge issue,

00:28:19.399 --> 00:28:25.610
also explained a kind of political paralysis
that didn't necessarily displace the importance

00:28:25.610 --> 00:28:31.340
of concerns about political -- about corruption
and reform and all those other things, at

00:28:31.340 --> 00:28:35.700
least not permanently, but managed to do so
in the short term.

00:28:35.700 --> 00:28:38.919
On the second question, was Saudi Arabia a
little affected?

00:28:38.919 --> 00:28:40.980
I actually don't agree with this.

00:28:40.980 --> 00:28:44.380
I think that Saudi Arabia was deeply affected
by the events of 2011.

00:28:44.380 --> 00:28:52.419
And if we look at the way that Abdullah managed
the issues in March, we see an important recalibration

00:28:52.419 --> 00:28:57.259
of Saudi domestic politics and the various
political forces that are often at play there.

00:28:57.259 --> 00:29:01.340
Most importantly was not the spending of money,
was not necessarily the mobilization of certain

00:29:01.340 --> 00:29:02.590
patronage networks.

00:29:02.590 --> 00:29:04.179
It's how the money was spent.

00:29:04.179 --> 00:29:09.600
Abdullah announced what would amount to a
$140 billion bailout package for Saudi institutions

00:29:09.600 --> 00:29:14.870
and citizens in March as a way to preempt,
and bonuses to public employees -- convinced

00:29:14.870 --> 00:29:19.639
businessmen to give similar kinds of bonuses
to those in the private sector.

00:29:19.639 --> 00:29:25.950
But most importantly was the redistribution
in intensified fashion of money to religious

00:29:25.950 --> 00:29:32.950
institutions, accommodating not only institutions
like the religious police but as well as others

00:29:32.950 --> 00:29:38.490
-- talking about tens of millions of dollars
being re-funneled into old Islamist networks

00:29:38.490 --> 00:29:43.539
that had enjoyed some favor in the 1980s and
early 1990s but that had increasingly come

00:29:43.539 --> 00:29:45.769
under pressure in the leadership of Abdullah.

00:29:45.769 --> 00:29:51.680
If we think about Saudi Arabia's modern history
as characterized in phases, its most important

00:29:51.680 --> 00:29:54.330
modern moment was 1979.

00:29:54.330 --> 00:29:59.539
Events in the region, as well as events at
home, put the al-Saud under tremendous pressure.

00:29:59.539 --> 00:30:04.991
Particularly one moment in November, when
Sunni radicals seized Mecca mosque, the state

00:30:04.991 --> 00:30:09.620
was put in a defensive position where it felt
like it had to accommodate Islamists in order

00:30:09.620 --> 00:30:12.409
to preempt any kind of Islamist revolution.

00:30:12.409 --> 00:30:16.789
Over the course of the 1980s and early 1990s,
this meant moving money out of development

00:30:16.789 --> 00:30:21.580
projects and other kinds of consumer projects
and into Islamist networks and projects -- jihad

00:30:21.580 --> 00:30:26.630
in Afghanistan but also the support for charitable
and other kinds of intellectual and religious

00:30:26.630 --> 00:30:28.039
networks at home.

00:30:28.039 --> 00:30:31.290
The result was that by the end of the 1980s,
there were hundreds of thousands of Saudi

00:30:31.290 --> 00:30:35.320
men who had been educated and trained in the
Islamic arts, in the Islamic sciences, to

00:30:35.320 --> 00:30:38.830
the detriment of things like business and
more technical fields.

00:30:38.830 --> 00:30:43.250
But it also meant the politicization of Islam
in important ways.

00:30:43.250 --> 00:30:49.500
Saudi Arabia is fully realized, I think, as
an Islamic state only in the 1980s and early

00:30:49.500 --> 00:30:50.500
1990s.

00:30:50.500 --> 00:30:56.070
When Abdullah became king in 2005, he initially
took several bold measures in order to undo

00:30:56.070 --> 00:30:57.649
that arrangement.

00:30:57.649 --> 00:31:02.210
He announced that Islamic law in the courts
would be codified; they'd be systematized

00:31:02.210 --> 00:31:04.389
under the direct leadership of the state.

00:31:04.389 --> 00:31:08.940
He made high-profile decisions about personnel,
on the Supreme Islamic Council, as well as

00:31:08.940 --> 00:31:12.820
within the religious police, and seemed to
indicate that he was willing to pursue if

00:31:12.820 --> 00:31:19.700
not what we might call a more liberal political
path, at least one that was softer on issues

00:31:19.700 --> 00:31:22.890
of tolerance and the importance of Islam more
generally.

00:31:22.890 --> 00:31:26.590
All of that was undone earlier this year in
the context of the bailout package.

00:31:26.590 --> 00:31:32.090
So in many ways, what I'm arguing is that
Saudi Arabia return to a familiar playbook,

00:31:32.090 --> 00:31:36.211
one that was first mapped out in the late
1970s in response to an earlier moment of

00:31:36.211 --> 00:31:37.211
domestic crisis.

00:31:37.211 --> 00:31:42.690
So Saudi Arabia is now sort of playing a game
that it played in the early 1980s and throughout

00:31:42.690 --> 00:31:48.539
that decade and that also produced all kinds
of pathologies and sets of political sort

00:31:48.539 --> 00:31:52.690
of tendencies that we should be concerned
about, not the least of which was the rise

00:31:52.690 --> 00:31:55.870
of militancy, sectarianism and all that other
stuff.

00:31:55.870 --> 00:31:59.620
Right, so I don't agree that Saudi Arabia
was little affected.

00:31:59.620 --> 00:32:05.029
It's true that the kingdom didn't enjoy or
experience mobilization and that Saudi citizens

00:32:05.029 --> 00:32:10.529
haven't come to realize political rights or
opportunity, but the state has undergone a

00:32:10.529 --> 00:32:17.070
significant transition back to one that -- to
something that resembles, you know, an earlier

00:32:17.070 --> 00:32:20.330
moment and one that we should be concerned
about.

00:32:20.330 --> 00:32:24.560
The second set of concerns -- and I'll deal
with this very quickly -- has to do with the

00:32:24.560 --> 00:32:26.350
U.S.- Saudi relationship.

00:32:26.350 --> 00:32:31.440
How should we think about Saudi Arabia and
its alliance -- or the way -- how should the

00:32:31.440 --> 00:32:35.929
U.S. think about its approach to Saudi Arabia
in the region?

00:32:35.929 --> 00:32:39.809
And here, too, Greg has outlined I think a
number of ways that we have historically thought

00:32:39.809 --> 00:32:44.960
about Saudi Arabia and that seem sort of pressing
in the moment, two of which I think are transparent

00:32:44.960 --> 00:32:46.370
and very clear.

00:32:46.370 --> 00:32:48.220
One is on the issue of nonproliferation.

00:32:48.220 --> 00:32:53.059
Clearly, it's not in the United States's interests
to see Saudi Arabia follow the path of Pakistan

00:32:53.059 --> 00:32:57.509
or Iran, if Iran is in fact pursuing nuclear
weapons.

00:32:57.509 --> 00:32:58.509
And the other is counterterrorism.

00:32:58.509 --> 00:33:02.899
The second of the third is counterterrorism,
where I think American and Saudi interests

00:33:02.899 --> 00:33:08.629
have very clearly aligned since at least 2003,
when Saudi Arabia was forced to deal with

00:33:08.629 --> 00:33:12.570
its own phenomenon of domestic terrorism.

00:33:12.570 --> 00:33:16.899
The third, though, I think, is less clear
to me, and it's less clear to me, although

00:33:16.899 --> 00:33:21.590
it enjoys a kind of hegemonic status within
the analytical community.

00:33:21.590 --> 00:33:26.159
And that's that the United States and Saudi
Arabia see the question of regional security

00:33:26.159 --> 00:33:27.809
in the same terms.

00:33:27.809 --> 00:33:33.000
Greg outlined one approach to this, and that
was countering or containing Iranian ambition

00:33:33.000 --> 00:33:34.320
and hegemony in the region.

00:33:34.320 --> 00:33:39.019
And it's certainly true that the U.S. and
Saudi Arabia agree that Iran has the potential

00:33:39.019 --> 00:33:42.830
to do great harm, and that it seeks -- and
that the Saudis and the Iranians are engaged

00:33:42.830 --> 00:33:44.190
in a balance of power struggle.

00:33:44.190 --> 00:33:50.750
And the U.S. would like to see Iran not win
that -- not win that competition.

00:33:50.750 --> 00:33:52.490
But there's more to security than this.

00:33:52.490 --> 00:33:57.659
There's more to security for the Americans,
and there's more to security for the Saudis.

00:33:57.659 --> 00:34:01.580
Greg mentioned that I've been an advocate
of sort of making sense of Saudi Arabia's

00:34:01.580 --> 00:34:04.409
response to the Arab Spring through the lens
of counterrevolution.

00:34:04.409 --> 00:34:07.830
I think this is a semantic difference between
us.

00:34:07.830 --> 00:34:12.350
And I don't think -- and I think Greg sort
of not unfairly but I think mistakenly concludes

00:34:12.350 --> 00:34:18.500
that by calling Saudi Arabia a "counterrevolutionary
power," that I necessarily attributed the

00:34:18.500 --> 00:34:20.700
ability to accomplish those things.

00:34:20.700 --> 00:34:26.090
Success shouldn't necessarily be conflated
with its desire to achieve a certain political

00:34:26.090 --> 00:34:27.090
outcome.

00:34:27.090 --> 00:34:30.030
So what is it that Saudi Arabia wants to achieve
in the Middle East?

00:34:30.030 --> 00:34:33.000
Yes, it supported the overthrow of Qadhafi.

00:34:33.000 --> 00:34:37.280
But much like Gates -- you know, Robert Gates
said about Libya, it was relatively easy for

00:34:37.280 --> 00:34:40.880
the U.S. to make a decision about intervention
in Libya because it's pretty far removed from

00:34:40.880 --> 00:34:42.610
our core area of interests.

00:34:42.610 --> 00:34:44.860
The same is true for Saudi Arabia.

00:34:44.860 --> 00:34:46.940
Qadhafi wasn't loved in Riyadh.

00:34:46.940 --> 00:34:51.140
It doesn't necessarily change the balance
of power either in the Levant or in the Persian

00:34:51.140 --> 00:34:53.150
Gulf for the Saudis to have supported his
overthrow.

00:34:53.150 --> 00:34:58.350
So, you know, I don't necessarily agree that
just because Saudi Arabia promoted or supported

00:34:58.350 --> 00:35:02.450
regime change there that it isn't in fact
a counterrevolutionary power.

00:35:02.450 --> 00:35:06.650
Closer to home -- and I do think Bahrain is
a very clear example where Saudi Arabia has

00:35:06.650 --> 00:35:12.350
been -- but even in Yemen and Syria, where
Saudi Arabia has allegedly been behind part

00:35:12.350 --> 00:35:16.920
of the negotiations to have Saleh step down,
and it remains to be seen if he'll actually

00:35:16.920 --> 00:35:21.680
do so or on what terms he'll actually do so
-- as well as in Syria -- my argument would

00:35:21.680 --> 00:35:28.520
be not that the Saudis have supported a transition
of power; rather, they have specifically supported

00:35:28.520 --> 00:35:32.680
the preservation of a certain kind of either
political status quo, as in the case of Yemen

00:35:32.680 --> 00:35:35.460
-- the Saudis don't want to see democracy
or reform come to Yemen.

00:35:35.460 --> 00:35:37.960
They want to see somebody like Saleh take
over there.

00:35:37.960 --> 00:35:41.020
It's the same "Mubarakism" without Mubarak.

00:35:41.020 --> 00:35:46.280
And in Syria, this has little to do with what
kind of political system follows the fall

00:35:46.280 --> 00:35:47.280
of Assad.

00:35:47.280 --> 00:35:51.940
It has everything to do with, I think, as
Greg rightly pointed out, the ability to check

00:35:51.940 --> 00:35:54.990
Syria off the potential box of Saudi friends
in the region.

00:35:54.990 --> 00:35:58.750
It has very little to do with the kinds of
political structures -- structures of power

00:35:58.750 --> 00:36:04.060
-- that will take place after Assad goes away,
if he in fact does.

00:36:04.060 --> 00:36:09.170
So if Saudi Arabia's a status quo power and
if the Arab Spring suggests to us that the

00:36:09.170 --> 00:36:12.830
political status quo was no longer viable
and that autocratic regimes are inherently

00:36:12.830 --> 00:36:18.150
unstable -- and maybe the Arab Spring will
hasten all of this, the unfolding of autocratic

00:36:18.150 --> 00:36:23.290
regimes elsewhere -- I mean, this seems to
me to be not a key or an indicator of future

00:36:23.290 --> 00:36:28.870
stability but, rather, the -- rather the sort
of permanent realization or pursuit of an

00:36:28.870 --> 00:36:34.450
inherently unstable arrangement of regional
alliances that may only be stable for six

00:36:34.450 --> 00:36:36.300
months to a year at a time.

00:36:36.300 --> 00:36:39.810
That's not the kind of place that I think
the U.S. should put its political emphasis

00:36:39.810 --> 00:36:42.800
or make its policy objectives.

00:36:42.800 --> 00:36:48.170
The last thing I want to talk about -- with
respect to security, Greg mentioned oil and

00:36:48.170 --> 00:36:53.960
sort of the importance of oil in Saudi Arabia's
shifting nature as an oil power.

00:36:53.960 --> 00:37:01.320
I happen to think that when we talk about
security, historically what the U.S. has emphasized

00:37:01.320 --> 00:37:06.490
since the mid-20th century by energy security
is that it seeks to protect the flow of oil

00:37:06.490 --> 00:37:07.490
from the Persian Gulf.

00:37:07.490 --> 00:37:08.490
Right?

00:37:08.490 --> 00:37:13.340
I mean, there's a reason that when Iran threatens
to close the Straits of Hormuz, it sort of

00:37:13.340 --> 00:37:18.670
evokes this response not only from many of
us but from policymakers that this is a terrible

00:37:18.670 --> 00:37:19.670
thing.

00:37:19.670 --> 00:37:20.670
And it is.

00:37:20.670 --> 00:37:23.440
I mean, if Iran was actually interested, or
able, in closing the Straits of Hormuz, don't

00:37:23.440 --> 00:37:26.680
get me wrong, global oil markets would be
devastated, at least in the short term.

00:37:26.680 --> 00:37:30.210
I know it would prove problematic for the
global economy, as well as for the American

00:37:30.210 --> 00:37:35.700
economy, in a whole host of related ways.

00:37:35.700 --> 00:37:43.670
But is in fact Saudi Arabia's commitment when
it comes to regional energy security to making

00:37:43.670 --> 00:37:49.080
sure that oil in sufficient quantities gets
out of the Persian Gulf and to global markets.

00:37:49.080 --> 00:37:52.040
This has long been its claim and this has
long been our claim.

00:37:52.040 --> 00:37:56.010
And I'll just raise this as a point of discussion
that's maybe worth following up, so that I

00:37:56.010 --> 00:37:57.330
don't belabor it too long.

00:37:57.330 --> 00:38:04.320
The reality is is that not only in the last
five years but since the 1970s, Saudi Arabia

00:38:04.320 --> 00:38:12.740
has not in fact been practicing or participating
in the provision of oil in sufficient quantities

00:38:12.740 --> 00:38:14.940
to stabilize global oil prices.

00:38:14.940 --> 00:38:18.140
Rather, it's been engaged in the manufacture
of oil scarcity.

00:38:18.140 --> 00:38:22.380
Resources scarcity is something that guides
much of our thinking, and I used to be a believer

00:38:22.380 --> 00:38:26.130
in peak oil and that this was a -- this was
a resource that would quickly disappear.

00:38:26.130 --> 00:38:29.630
I was convinced that that's -- you know, basically
through empirical evidence -- that's really

00:38:29.630 --> 00:38:35.300
not true, and that what Saudi Arabia and many
of its oil producers engage in is a sort of

00:38:35.300 --> 00:38:39.890
restrictive approach or a limited approach
to global oil markets in which they're looking

00:38:39.890 --> 00:38:47.290
not for sort of stability or security in the
sense that we think about; rather, they're

00:38:47.290 --> 00:38:52.250
thinking about the maximum price that they
can achieve in order to maintain global dependence

00:38:52.250 --> 00:38:56.670
on oil, which means that they limit the quantity
of oil that get to the market.

00:38:56.670 --> 00:39:03.420
And the reality is that, absent all other
factors, Saudi Arabia, Iran, Iraq, Kuwait,

00:39:03.420 --> 00:39:07.850
the Emirates, Qatar, Oman and the other oil
producers in the region are in a much more

00:39:07.850 --> 00:39:12.040
delicate position than the United States is
in the Persian Gulf, even though it seems

00:39:12.040 --> 00:39:13.320
the opposite is true.

00:39:13.320 --> 00:39:14.320
Why?

00:39:14.320 --> 00:39:19.410
Because they have to sell their oil in order
to maintain their political systems at home.

00:39:19.410 --> 00:39:24.050
They're under incredible scrutiny and pressure
to maintain a price structure that allows

00:39:24.050 --> 00:39:29.040
them to maintain all of the elaborate political
systems that Greg outlined.

00:39:29.040 --> 00:39:34.370
And in fact American militarism, the presence
of our military in significant numbers in

00:39:34.370 --> 00:39:38.240
the Persian Gulf provides them cover to manufacture
scarcity.

00:39:38.240 --> 00:39:39.610
It doesn't work the other way around.

00:39:39.610 --> 00:39:43.690
We're not providing them cover to make sure
that oil gets to market in sufficient quantities;

00:39:43.690 --> 00:39:46.640
in fact, we provide them cover to do the opposite.

00:39:46.640 --> 00:39:52.270
So if this is true -- and I think it's open
for some discussion -- if it is true, then

00:39:52.270 --> 00:39:56.700
what security means to us in the region is
very different from what it means to Saudi

00:39:56.700 --> 00:40:01.950
Arabia, and it should be thought of as something
that's worth criticizing and scrutinizing,

00:40:01.950 --> 00:40:05.570
rather than assuming what's true today might
have been true three decades ago.

00:40:05.570 --> 00:40:06.830
I'll stop there.

00:40:06.830 --> 00:40:07.830
&gt;&gt;STARES: Great.

00:40:07.830 --> 00:40:12.600
Thanks very much, Toby, for that contrasting
assessment of Saudi Arabia.

00:40:12.600 --> 00:40:15.460
We have about 50 minutes of -- for Q&amp;A.

00:40:15.460 --> 00:40:21.540
Those of you who want to ask a question, do
the usual thing; wave your placard there.

00:40:21.540 --> 00:40:23.560
I see Judith has already offered to start.

00:40:23.560 --> 00:40:27.380
I will try to get you in the order that you
put them up.

00:40:27.380 --> 00:40:31.450
Those in the bleachers should wave frantically
if they want to be recognized.

00:40:31.450 --> 00:40:35.030
And I will try to call on you.

00:40:35.030 --> 00:40:40.080
So Judith, take it away.

00:40:40.080 --> 00:40:45.590
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: Just a quick point to Toby.

00:40:45.590 --> 00:40:51.690
I'm not sure I could go with the oil scarcity
theory, but I think we also need to recognize

00:40:51.690 --> 00:40:56.780
that Saudi Arabia's in the middle of an energy
crisis of its own, which is why they're going

00:40:56.780 --> 00:40:58.760
for alternative and nuclear.

00:40:58.760 --> 00:41:02.650
They're using today 4 million barrels a day
for domestic purposes.

00:41:02.650 --> 00:41:05.930
Their population doubles every 20 years.

00:41:05.930 --> 00:41:11.040
They're going to be using 9 million within
10 years, and that means they have no income.

00:41:11.040 --> 00:41:18.130
So you know, the question of what's going
on and how they're selling their oil I think

00:41:18.130 --> 00:41:22.130
deserves to be examined very, very closely.

00:41:22.130 --> 00:41:27.170
And Greg, excellent report and so on, but
I think in your presentation -- I haven't

00:41:27.170 --> 00:41:31.410
had a chance to fully devour all of the report
yet.

00:41:31.410 --> 00:41:33.590
You may talk about it there.

00:41:33.590 --> 00:41:40.600
I think you can't talk about Saudi -- you
know, promoting sectarianism (Iraq ?) and

00:41:40.600 --> 00:41:49.050
Bahrain without recognizing that between 2000
and 2008, they had very serious problems with

00:41:49.050 --> 00:41:57.310
the U.S., the catastrophe of, I think, the
Iraq War, the unequivocal, unconditional choice

00:41:57.310 --> 00:42:04.030
of Maliki by the U.S. and the continued support
for Maliki, who the king doesn't trust -- thinks

00:42:04.030 --> 00:42:09.240
he lied to him and that he's an agent of Iran,
some of which may be true, some of which is

00:42:09.240 --> 00:42:11.620
not true -- and also Bahrain.

00:42:11.620 --> 00:42:19.060
I mean, when Bahrain blew, it seems to me
that the United States and Saudi Arabia made

00:42:19.060 --> 00:42:21.490
really catastrophic mistakes.

00:42:21.490 --> 00:42:26.570
I mean, that was the moment for the U.S. to
call up the king and tell him the prime minister

00:42:26.570 --> 00:42:28.702
has to go; let the crown prince do a dialogue.

00:42:28.702 --> 00:42:34.500
And I think the violence could have been avoided
because I think the Khalifa family has some

00:42:34.500 --> 00:42:38.270
very, very important limitations.

00:42:38.270 --> 00:42:40.950
They've ruined the way the government has
run.

00:42:40.950 --> 00:42:45.220
And there are some very real issues in Bahrain
that could have been handled.

00:42:45.220 --> 00:42:50.250
They can't now, but they could have been,
but we -- you know, we don't have a Middle

00:42:50.250 --> 00:42:51.540
East policy.

00:42:51.540 --> 00:42:56.110
I would say in terms of threat, of course
they're petrified.

00:42:56.110 --> 00:43:02.320
They're so allergic to Shi'ite you can't even
mention it to the Ph.D.s.

00:43:02.320 --> 00:43:07.220
You know, I'm sure you've had that experience,
so there's no point even to talk about it.

00:43:07.220 --> 00:43:10.690
Iran of course scares them to death.

00:43:10.690 --> 00:43:14.630
But I think that the biggest threat to Saudi
Arabia is demographic.

00:43:14.630 --> 00:43:19.250
They're old guys, succession, et cetera.

00:43:19.250 --> 00:43:22.650
They simply cannot handle the demography.

00:43:22.650 --> 00:43:29.330
As I said, population doubles every 20 years;
seven children average per woman; 60 percent

00:43:29.330 --> 00:43:34.010
under the age of 20; 40 percent under the
age of 13.

00:43:34.010 --> 00:43:42.240
And even if oil is $300 a barrel, they cannot
keep them down and out and quiet by paying

00:43:42.240 --> 00:43:43.360
them off.

00:43:43.360 --> 00:43:50.700
So how they handle their education is the
key to the security and stability in the future.

00:43:50.700 --> 00:43:53.300
&gt;&gt;STARES: All right.

00:43:53.300 --> 00:43:55.960
I want you to digest that.

00:43:55.960 --> 00:44:00.870
Do you want to -- (off mic) -- or do you want
to respond?

00:44:00.870 --> 00:44:01.870
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: Yeah, yeah, sure.

00:44:01.870 --> 00:44:06.770
I mean, I think this demographic issue is
fascinating because I think for years the

00:44:06.770 --> 00:44:11.060
Saudis lied about their population, right,
and they exaggerated it, so we thought there

00:44:11.060 --> 00:44:13.070
were more Saudis than there actually were.

00:44:13.070 --> 00:44:17.630
Now it seems we're getting down to a relatively
reliable number.

00:44:17.630 --> 00:44:18.630
Right?

00:44:18.630 --> 00:44:22.940
About 30 million now, 22 million of them Saudis.

00:44:22.940 --> 00:44:25.180
The demographics are not great.

00:44:25.180 --> 00:44:26.540
There's no question about it.

00:44:26.540 --> 00:44:33.110
But they probably aren't as bad as many people
think they are, because we tend to think back

00:44:33.110 --> 00:44:39.260
to the inflated figures that they pushed up
until the '90s.

00:44:39.260 --> 00:44:42.380
But without a doubt, there's a demographic
issue here.

00:44:42.380 --> 00:44:49.020
How they deal with it, of course, is -- the
approach from the last oil crash of the late

00:44:49.020 --> 00:44:52.240
'90s was we're going to let the private sector
do this.

00:44:52.240 --> 00:44:57.620
And the Saudi private sector has become an
extremely powerful creator of jobs.

00:44:57.620 --> 00:45:04.440
Unfortunately, all those jobs go to foreigners,
and that's part of the deal in Saudi Arabia.

00:45:04.440 --> 00:45:05.440
Right?

00:45:05.440 --> 00:45:12.620
Unless the Saudi government can do a political
deal with its private sector, which is a dependent

00:45:12.620 --> 00:45:19.030
private sector but a much more sophisticated
and interesting one than in the oil boom of

00:45:19.030 --> 00:45:26.440
the '70s, and a much more globalized one -- unless
the Saudis can get this deal done, where they

00:45:26.440 --> 00:45:32.920
can increase the price of foreign labor and
thus increase the incentives for their own

00:45:32.920 --> 00:45:37.530
private sector to hire Saudis, train them
-- because they're not being trained in the

00:45:37.530 --> 00:45:43.890
schools -- and give them jobs, then I think
that that's a huge problem.

00:45:43.890 --> 00:45:45.810
It's not that people don't realize this.

00:45:45.810 --> 00:45:47.340
People talk about this all the time.

00:45:47.340 --> 00:45:50.260
It's a question of political will, and it
hasn't been summoned yet.

00:45:50.260 --> 00:45:51.720
&gt;&gt;JONES: But there's no mechanism.

00:45:51.720 --> 00:45:53.040
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: Well, there's a mechanism.

00:45:53.040 --> 00:45:57.100
The king could -- the mechanism in Saudi Arabia
is the king says, and it happens, right?

00:45:57.100 --> 00:45:58.210
&gt;&gt;JONES: Well, no.

00:45:58.210 --> 00:46:00.410
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: Except for codification of Shari'a
--

00:46:00.410 --> 00:46:01.740
&gt;&gt;JONES: Which hasn't happened.

00:46:01.740 --> 00:46:03.360
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: -- which he says -- and it hasn't
happened.

00:46:03.360 --> 00:46:06.690
&gt;&gt;JONES: I mean, there's no pressure on the
royal family to take these steps.

00:46:06.690 --> 00:46:09.200
I mean, I don't think there's a sense -- I
think there's a -- there's a recognition --

00:46:09.200 --> 00:46:11.770
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: Everybody talks about this all the
time.

00:46:11.770 --> 00:46:13.572
&gt;&gt;JONES: Well, then -- you know, then they're
not rational actors, right?

00:46:13.572 --> 00:46:15.390
And we know this -- that because they won't
--

00:46:15.390 --> 00:46:16.390
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: Well, no, they're short-term actors.

00:46:16.390 --> 00:46:19.950
&gt;&gt;JONES: -- they won't very clearly sort of
undertake what's necessary.

00:46:19.950 --> 00:46:21.590
I'm not sure education's the answer.

00:46:21.590 --> 00:46:25.120
I mean, we're -- I mean, that's a cultural
sort of -- you know, that's offering up a

00:46:25.120 --> 00:46:29.900
cultural explanation that if we could retrain
them in certain ways that they might find

00:46:29.900 --> 00:46:34.370
their way forward to more serious and sort
of what might be more recognizable forms of

00:46:34.370 --> 00:46:35.940
development and economic practice.

00:46:35.940 --> 00:46:37.690
The reality is this is all political.

00:46:37.690 --> 00:46:41.520
The Saudis were very calculated about how
they both structured the political/economic

00:46:41.520 --> 00:46:44.200
system but also how people seek to siphon
rents off of it.

00:46:44.200 --> 00:46:48.560
I mean, this is -- this is completely reasonable
behavior, given the centrality of oil.

00:46:48.560 --> 00:46:50.550
I mean, I'm not going to speak for Greg.

00:46:50.550 --> 00:46:52.690
But I mean, I think the Saudis are stuck.

00:46:52.690 --> 00:46:58.370
I think they're fundamentally stuck in a dilemma
that they cannot figure a way out of.

00:46:58.370 --> 00:47:01.100
And they don't have the political will to
do it, which means it's going to come about

00:47:01.100 --> 00:47:02.310
spectacularly at some point.

00:47:02.310 --> 00:47:07.010
It may not be five years from now, but eventually.

00:47:07.010 --> 00:47:08.010
&gt;&gt;STARES: Michael?

00:47:08.010 --> 00:47:12.170
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: Panelists barely touched on
the subject of succession.

00:47:12.170 --> 00:47:19.730
Let's accept Greg's definition that the -- that
the real serious issue to talk about is the

00:47:19.730 --> 00:47:22.940
succession to the next generation of leaders.

00:47:22.940 --> 00:47:32.550
I'd like -- I'd be interested in the panelists'
comments on how successful that new generation

00:47:32.550 --> 00:47:41.400
is in terms of the very complicated set of
political skills that Gregory outlined as

00:47:41.400 --> 00:47:50.220
being essential to stability if we want to
accept that just for a moment in Saudi Arabia.

00:47:50.220 --> 00:47:53.560
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: The next generation is too big to
generalize about.

00:47:53.560 --> 00:47:57.010
I mean, there's incredibly competent people
and interesting people.

00:47:57.010 --> 00:47:58.690
There are wastrels.

00:47:58.690 --> 00:48:05.700
There are people who would -- who we would
consider to be radical extremists.

00:48:05.700 --> 00:48:10.260
And there are people who would be much more
comfortable in Mayfair or in Georgetown than

00:48:10.260 --> 00:48:12.700
they are in their own country.

00:48:12.700 --> 00:48:18.420
It is such a diverse group that I'm very uncomfortable
in generalizing about it.

00:48:18.420 --> 00:48:22.950
The only thing that I would say is I've never
met a single one of them -- and I've met some

00:48:22.950 --> 00:48:28.900
of them -- who doesn't think that it's their
family's right to rule the country.

00:48:28.900 --> 00:48:36.220
They are -- they're not political reformers
the way we would understand political reform,

00:48:36.220 --> 00:48:39.800
it seems to me.

00:48:39.800 --> 00:48:43.470
My bottom line on succession is that those
who talk about it don't know about it, and

00:48:43.470 --> 00:48:45.100
those who know about it don't talk about it.

00:48:45.100 --> 00:48:49.530
So I'll prove that I don't know about it by
talking a little bit about it.

00:48:49.530 --> 00:48:58.010
And it seems to me that there's an untested
-- you know, that they've -- trying to institutionalize

00:48:58.010 --> 00:49:03.000
it with this allegiance commission, the Bay'ah
committee, commission, but this is a completely

00:49:03.000 --> 00:49:06.160
untested institution.

00:49:06.160 --> 00:49:10.540
And the smoothest way that this would happen
is while the old guys, the remnants of the

00:49:10.540 --> 00:49:21.160
old guys are alive, that they do a deal and
that that deal then gets ratified by this

00:49:21.160 --> 00:49:27.760
new institution and that in that deal they
make clear that there's a number of lines

00:49:27.760 --> 00:49:34.640
in the family that are going to continue to
have an important role.

00:49:34.640 --> 00:49:36.880
But this is also a question of political will.

00:49:36.880 --> 00:49:40.310
Toby talked about how they -- you know, they
don't have the political will.

00:49:40.310 --> 00:49:44.790
If they have the political will to do this,
I think that the next generation's succession

00:49:44.790 --> 00:49:46.650
could be smooth.

00:49:46.650 --> 00:49:53.480
But if they keep kicking the can down the
road and then they all die, then I don't know.

00:49:53.480 --> 00:49:57.590
I mean, it's a wide -- it's wide open at that
point.

00:49:57.590 --> 00:50:02.150
The Saudis have been pretty good at dealing
with succession since the problems of the

00:50:02.150 --> 00:50:05.620
late '50s and early '60s, which was very touch
and go.

00:50:05.620 --> 00:50:10.710
Toby said 1979 as the turning point of modern
Saudi history; frankly, I think the late '50s

00:50:10.710 --> 00:50:15.280
and early '60s was the most dangerous time
for the al-Saud family.

00:50:15.280 --> 00:50:20.180
They were beset by regional -- real regional
enemies in Nasser's pan-Arabism.

00:50:20.180 --> 00:50:26.520
They had a civil war going on in Yemen from
the early '60s.

00:50:26.520 --> 00:50:29.900
And they were profoundly split domestically.

00:50:29.900 --> 00:50:34.940
And they had as much opposition I think domestically,
and perhaps even at a more elite level, than

00:50:34.940 --> 00:50:37.080
they have now.

00:50:37.080 --> 00:50:41.740
And, you know, they go through that through
luck.

00:50:41.740 --> 00:50:48.620
And if this generation remembers that dicey
time, the coming generation, maybe they'll

00:50:48.620 --> 00:50:51.670
be able to work it out.

00:50:51.670 --> 00:50:53.560
But I don't make any predictions on that.

00:50:53.560 --> 00:50:55.570
&gt;&gt;STARES: Toby, anything you want to add on
-- OK.

00:50:55.570 --> 00:51:00.650
I think -- (name inaudible) -- and then Elise.

00:51:00.650 --> 00:51:02.830
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: Thank you.

00:51:02.830 --> 00:51:06.900
Well, thank you to both of our presenters,
and thank you to Paul for convening such a

00:51:06.900 --> 00:51:12.280
stimulating and really excellent panel here.

00:51:12.280 --> 00:51:17.290
It seems that since the Arab uprisings began,
we've seen a different type of Saudi foreign

00:51:17.290 --> 00:51:22.250
policy if -- in style if not in substance.

00:51:22.250 --> 00:51:24.300
The objectives may be status quo.

00:51:24.300 --> 00:51:26.840
They may be counterrevolutionary.

00:51:26.840 --> 00:51:32.250
But at least it seems to be a style of activism
that we've not seen before -- be it the approach

00:51:32.250 --> 00:51:40.000
it took vis-a-vis Qadhafi, the approach it
took in Bahrain, the approach it's taken elsewhere

00:51:40.000 --> 00:51:45.060
in calling for the U.N. to intervene in various
conflicts.

00:51:45.060 --> 00:51:53.070
One, is this in fact a phenomenon, or is this
just an ahistorical observation?

00:51:53.070 --> 00:52:00.410
And if indeed it is true, is this a function
and a reaction to a perceived absence of American

00:52:00.410 --> 00:52:02.670
activism and role?

00:52:02.670 --> 00:52:07.190
Or how do you explain it, if in fact -- an
accurate description of reality?

00:52:07.190 --> 00:52:13.240
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: Yeah, you know, I think that this
activism actually predates the Arab Spring.

00:52:13.240 --> 00:52:16.170
It's just they've failed on most of their
initiatives.

00:52:16.170 --> 00:52:21.480
I mean, I think that there are three areas
where -- once the king decided -- '07 -- '06,

00:52:21.480 --> 00:52:27.880
'07 that he couldn't do any deals with the
Iranians and they were not to be trusted,

00:52:27.880 --> 00:52:32.940
I think he made three initiatives, all of
which failed, right, all aimed at rolling

00:52:32.940 --> 00:52:35.590
back Iranian influence in the Arab world.

00:52:35.590 --> 00:52:38.910
One was in Iraq -- very strong support for
Allawi.

00:52:38.910 --> 00:52:42.810
He backed Allawi in '05 but strong support
for Allawi.

00:52:42.810 --> 00:52:50.040
The second was Lebanon -- strong support for
Hariri in March 14, and in Palestine trying

00:52:50.040 --> 00:52:59.660
to bring Hamas and Fatah together in an effort
to, you know, cut off this Iranian-Hamas developing

00:52:59.660 --> 00:53:00.660
relationship.

00:53:00.660 --> 00:53:01.660
He failed on every one of those.

00:53:01.660 --> 00:53:06.480
You know, one of the jokes I tell is that,
you know, you can understand why the Saudis

00:53:06.480 --> 00:53:09.610
are so reluctant about democracy.

00:53:09.610 --> 00:53:11.690
I mean, you know, they back Allawi.

00:53:11.690 --> 00:53:15.320
He wins the elections, gets the most seats,
doesn't become prime minister.

00:53:15.320 --> 00:53:17.660
They back March 14.

00:53:17.660 --> 00:53:18.670
It wins the election.

00:53:18.670 --> 00:53:20.380
Hezbollah runs the government.

00:53:20.380 --> 00:53:22.140
Of course they don't think democracy works.

00:53:22.140 --> 00:53:26.420
When they pay for democracy and their guys
win, they don't get to rule.

00:53:26.420 --> 00:53:31.690
So I think that the Saudis have been more
active, but they've failed.

00:53:31.690 --> 00:53:33.250
They've failed.

00:53:33.250 --> 00:53:38.260
And one of the reasons they played up the
Houthis in Yemen so much, it seemed to be,

00:53:38.260 --> 00:53:40.950
is because they thought that they needed a
victory.

00:53:40.950 --> 00:53:46.290
You know, they're going to make this bunch
of guys in Yemen into clients of Iran.

00:53:46.290 --> 00:53:51.390
You know, they use Iranian revolutionary rhetoric,
but there's no great evidence that the Houthis

00:53:51.390 --> 00:53:53.180
are clients of Iran.

00:53:53.180 --> 00:53:55.650
So they make them into clients of Iran and
they go pound them.

00:53:55.650 --> 00:53:57.510
"We won one."

00:53:57.510 --> 00:54:02.290
And I think that that was a large impetus
behind the publicity that the Saudis gave

00:54:02.290 --> 00:54:03.640
to the Houthis.

00:54:03.640 --> 00:54:09.670
So I don't see this activism now -- in fact,
I see them in the Arab Spring as being incredibly

00:54:09.670 --> 00:54:11.930
discombobulated.

00:54:11.930 --> 00:54:14.200
Right?

00:54:14.200 --> 00:54:17.530
They weren't at the front on Libya; they were
in the back, and they came along.

00:54:17.530 --> 00:54:22.210
It took them months to decide that Bashar
was not their guy.

00:54:22.210 --> 00:54:30.490
You know, this innate desire for nothing to
change really was not -- they were not well-suited

00:54:30.490 --> 00:54:35.880
to deal with the events of 2011 because so
much was changing everywhere.

00:54:35.880 --> 00:54:42.370
I think the only new thing that we see in
2011 -- you know, and Toby and I here disagree.

00:54:42.370 --> 00:54:43.370
Right?

00:54:43.370 --> 00:54:50.190
The only new thing we see is a willingness
to use Saudi forces beyond their borders to

00:54:50.190 --> 00:54:53.910
protect regimes that they think are key to
them.

00:54:53.910 --> 00:54:56.540
Now, this isn't the first time Saudi troops
have been in Bahrain.

00:54:56.540 --> 00:54:57.540
Right?

00:54:57.540 --> 00:54:58.540
In the mid-'90s --

00:54:58.540 --> 00:54:59.540
&gt;&gt;JONES: '96.

00:54:59.540 --> 00:55:03.870
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: Right, when the GCC summit -- that
first -- that intifada -- the mid-'90s intifada

00:55:03.870 --> 00:55:04.870
started in Bahrain.

00:55:04.870 --> 00:55:06.970
The Saudis sent troops into Bahrain as well.

00:55:06.970 --> 00:55:07.970
Right?

00:55:07.970 --> 00:55:12.210
But the very public way with which it was
done, they made no secret about it.

00:55:12.210 --> 00:55:15.360
They were not apologetic about it.

00:55:15.360 --> 00:55:18.130
That is the only new thing that I see.

00:55:18.130 --> 00:55:19.950
&gt;&gt;JONES: Yeah.

00:55:19.950 --> 00:55:27.280
No, I do think that sort of the scope or the
breadth of Saudi activism is new; I mean,

00:55:27.280 --> 00:55:29.830
moving on multiple fronts at one time.

00:55:29.830 --> 00:55:34.310
And I'll address, Rob, the sort of second
part of your question, and that is: How does

00:55:34.310 --> 00:55:37.230
this -- what does this tell us about what
the Saudis think about the U.S.?

00:55:37.230 --> 00:55:40.440
I think the Saudis are in a difficult position.

00:55:40.440 --> 00:55:46.640
I think the way they think about this is that
they have a complicated place, that historically

00:55:46.640 --> 00:55:48.670
they believe they've had a special relationship.

00:55:48.670 --> 00:55:52.701
But since 2001 and, arguably, from the Clinton
administration, they felt like it's been a

00:55:52.701 --> 00:55:55.210
relationship under pressure in various ways.

00:55:55.210 --> 00:56:01.050
So they've been put in a position where they
have to balance that uncertainty with a kind

00:56:01.050 --> 00:56:04.630
of desperate sense that they want to preserve
it as best as possible.

00:56:04.630 --> 00:56:08.000
The Chinese aren't a reasonable alternative
to the U.S.

00:56:08.000 --> 00:56:13.400
The Chinese can't project kind of military
power, the political will to do the sorts

00:56:13.400 --> 00:56:15.990
of things that the U.S. has historically been
willing to do.

00:56:15.990 --> 00:56:21.180
But at the same time, as Judith pointed out,
the U.S. doesn't have a Middle East policy.

00:56:21.180 --> 00:56:22.730
We don't have a clear approach.

00:56:22.730 --> 00:56:26.090
We don't know what our priorities are, and
Andrew has said the same thing in multiple

00:56:26.090 --> 00:56:29.700
settings, right, that you know, it's kind
of ad hoc; it's convoluted.

00:56:29.700 --> 00:56:35.180
Are we committed to democracy at this point,
or are we a status quo power ourselves?

00:56:35.180 --> 00:56:39.030
Is Iran going to shape our policy, or it is
going to be some combination of things?

00:56:39.030 --> 00:56:43.461
I think the Saudis understand very clearly,
perhaps better than most, the uncertainty

00:56:43.461 --> 00:56:46.050
that characterizes the American position.

00:56:46.050 --> 00:56:49.130
And that activism that I think you've correctly
identified -- and I think Greg's right, too;

00:56:49.130 --> 00:56:57.050
it has its roots elsewhere but -- is partly
a reflection of a power in the Middle East

00:56:57.050 --> 00:56:58.510
that's got a lot of resources.

00:56:58.510 --> 00:57:04.090
It's got a lot of money, even if it's convoluted,
to be able to attempt to shape outcomes.

00:57:04.090 --> 00:57:10.610
Again, I wouldn't attribute to the Saudis
necessarily a level of success that they've

00:57:10.610 --> 00:57:12.840
been effective, but I think they're trying.

00:57:12.840 --> 00:57:15.540
&gt;&gt;STARES: OK, Elise and the Michelle (sp).

00:57:15.540 --> 00:57:17.970
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: Thanks so much, both of you,
for this really interesting discussion.

00:57:17.970 --> 00:57:20.990
And Greg, I really enjoyed the report.

00:57:20.990 --> 00:57:27.670
A just quick follow-up on the succession issue,
and then I -- a question about the Salafists.

00:57:27.670 --> 00:57:32.670
On the succession issue, I mean, I don't want
to call King Abdullah a reformer, but when

00:57:32.670 --> 00:57:33.670
I -- when I use --

00:57:33.670 --> 00:57:35.180
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: Not when you're sitting next to Toby
Jones, you don't, no.

00:57:35.180 --> 00:57:36.180
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: Exactly.

00:57:36.180 --> 00:57:42.130
But when I say that, you know, I think if
there was any anybody that thought that King

00:57:42.130 --> 00:57:48.460
Abdullah on the scales tipped more towards
reform than not -- and I think the death of

00:57:48.460 --> 00:57:54.160
the crown prince -- a lot of people were very
concerned that -- about Prince Nayef.

00:57:54.160 --> 00:58:02.430
And does that take Saudi Arabia -- you know,
instead of begrudgingly going along with small

00:58:02.430 --> 00:58:11.260
reforms, are we going to see under -- as we
move towards succession, are we moving even

00:58:11.260 --> 00:58:13.680
farther away from that?

00:58:13.680 --> 00:58:20.850
Or do you think that the new leadership eventually
will have to begrudgingly accept some more

00:58:20.850 --> 00:58:22.400
freedoms for the people?

00:58:22.400 --> 00:58:27.110
And then on the Salafists, I was wondering
if you could touch upon Saudi funding for

00:58:27.110 --> 00:58:33.020
some of these groups, political groups, throughout
the region.

00:58:33.020 --> 00:58:43.820
And does that -- does the rise of more Islamic
parties help Saudi consolidate its leadership

00:58:43.820 --> 00:58:45.360
position in the region?

00:58:45.360 --> 00:58:52.210
Or are we seeing in the region -- as we've
seen Qatar and Turkey take greater leadership

00:58:52.210 --> 00:58:56.570
roles in the region, is it too late for that?

00:58:56.570 --> 00:59:02.530
&gt;&gt;JONES: Let me just offer this up: My understanding
of the Saudi role in the -- in terms of who

00:59:02.530 --> 00:59:05.280
it's supporting and who it's not supporting,
they're not supporting the Salafis or the

00:59:05.280 --> 00:59:06.470
Islamists at all.

00:59:06.470 --> 00:59:07.470
The Saudis are --

00:59:07.470 --> 00:59:09.030
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: I was wondering.

00:59:09.030 --> 00:59:10.030
I thought maybe they were giving --

00:59:10.030 --> 00:59:12.360
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: I'm sure that they raise -- I'm sure
plenty of these Salafists -- everybody in

00:59:12.360 --> 00:59:13.360
Egypt --

00:59:13.360 --> 00:59:14.490
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: A lot of people -- a lot of
people are wondering where the money is coming

00:59:14.490 --> 00:59:15.490
from.

00:59:15.490 --> 00:59:17.820
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: Everybody in Egypt says all this
money for the Salafists come from Saudi.

00:59:17.820 --> 00:59:18.910
They never have any evidence.

00:59:18.910 --> 00:59:20.540
&gt;&gt;JONES: But state funding went to SCAF.

00:59:20.540 --> 00:59:23.640
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: Yeah, state funding is a different
story and not as -- but not as much as they

00:59:23.640 --> 00:59:24.640
promised.

00:59:24.640 --> 00:59:25.640
&gt;&gt;JONES: But the Saudis are --

00:59:25.640 --> 00:59:27.020
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: They promised ($)4 billion and they
probably haven't even given a billion.

00:59:27.020 --> 00:59:28.920
&gt;&gt;JONES: Right, well, it's only a billion.

00:59:28.920 --> 00:59:29.920
(Laughter.)

00:59:29.920 --> 00:59:33.590
The Saudis have been very clear, I mean, in
spite of Nayef's comments that we will maintain

00:59:33.590 --> 00:59:36.900
our commitment to Salifism -- which, by the
way, is a new thing.

00:59:36.900 --> 00:59:38.790
The Saudis haven't always called it Salifism
--

00:59:38.790 --> 00:59:39.790
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: Right.

00:59:39.790 --> 00:59:42.850
&gt;&gt;JONES: -- so this is kind of an innovation.

00:59:42.850 --> 00:59:47.530
The kingdom's position, historically, on the
Islamists, while they've cultivated relationships

00:59:47.530 --> 00:59:52.990
with some sectors of it, have been deeply
nervous about the power of Islam and the Muslim

00:59:52.990 --> 00:59:54.100
Brotherhood in particular.

00:59:54.100 --> 00:59:57.170
And that has to do with their own domestic
experience, as well as events in the region.

00:59:57.170 --> 01:00:01.710
I think they're -- you know, the obvious thing
might be to say in Egypt, with the success

01:00:01.710 --> 01:00:05.100
of the Islamists in the parliamentary elections,
is that the Saudis are pleased.

01:00:05.100 --> 01:00:09.610
I think the Saudis are in fact very anxious
and would like to see SCAF ultimately hold

01:00:09.610 --> 01:00:11.780
onto power and not give up.

01:00:11.780 --> 01:00:16.350
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: Yeah, I have no doubt that the Salafists
have networks into Saudi Arabia and raise

01:00:16.350 --> 01:00:18.350
money there.

01:00:18.350 --> 01:00:19.350
How could they not.

01:00:19.350 --> 01:00:20.350
Right?

01:00:20.350 --> 01:00:22.160
There are all sorts of sympathetic people
in Saudi Arabia.

01:00:22.160 --> 01:00:27.350
I get no indication that it's a government
policy to back the Salafists.

01:00:27.350 --> 01:00:32.010
And I think that -- I agree with Toby here;
I think that the Saudis are profoundly nervous

01:00:32.010 --> 01:00:37.480
about this, because if you get the whole -- if
you get the Salafist movement basically saying,

01:00:37.480 --> 01:00:44.080
"Yeah, democratic politics all good," that's
troublesome to them in a way that goes beyond

01:00:44.080 --> 01:00:45.420
the tactical.

01:00:45.420 --> 01:00:50.830
So, yeah, I think that, you know, the fact
that the Salafis in Egypt sometimes run up

01:00:50.830 --> 01:00:56.550
the Saudi flag at their rallies and all, it
must -- the king must see that on TV and go,

01:00:56.550 --> 01:01:03.310
"Good" -- (laughs) -- you know, but I don't
think that they're banking on the Salafists

01:01:03.310 --> 01:01:06.370
to be their lever in Egypt.

01:01:06.370 --> 01:01:11.830
And the Brothers, I mean, Nayef has publicly,
on more than one occasion, said the brothers

01:01:11.830 --> 01:01:15.890
are the cause of everything bad that's happened
in the Middle East.

01:01:15.890 --> 01:01:19.660
So he -- he doesn't like them at all.

01:01:19.660 --> 01:01:26.260
So the thing with the brothers is even more
complicated, in terms of how the Saudis view

01:01:26.260 --> 01:01:27.260
this.

01:01:27.260 --> 01:01:30.750
&gt;&gt;JONES: In fact, the most powerful potential
opposition force in Saudi Arabia is the Saudi

01:01:30.750 --> 01:01:32.410
analog to the Egyptian Muslim Brothers.

01:01:32.410 --> 01:01:33.410
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: Really?

01:01:33.410 --> 01:01:34.410
You think so?

01:01:34.410 --> 01:01:35.410
&gt;&gt;JONES: Well, I mean, well --

01:01:35.410 --> 01:01:36.410
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: I don't think so.

01:01:36.410 --> 01:01:38.120
&gt;&gt;JONES: You don't think that the sahwa still
has the potential capacity to mobilize?

01:01:38.120 --> 01:01:41.670
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: I mean, I think the sahwa only can
do that.

01:01:41.670 --> 01:01:45.100
This word, sahwa, awakening, is something
that gets thrown around in the Middle East

01:01:45.100 --> 01:01:46.100
all the time.

01:01:46.100 --> 01:01:47.600
Now, the Arab Spring is the sahwa.

01:01:47.600 --> 01:01:48.600
Right?

01:01:48.600 --> 01:01:50.400
And then there is the sahwa in Iraq, right,
where the Sunnis --

01:01:50.400 --> 01:01:51.570
&gt;&gt;JONES: Should've trademarked --

01:01:51.570 --> 01:01:55.890
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: -- somebody should've -- but the
sahwa in Saudi Arabia, which Toby has written

01:01:55.890 --> 01:02:04.700
about, and the best book on it is Stephane
Lacroix's book, was this kind of Muslim Brotherhood-influenced

01:02:04.700 --> 01:02:10.010
but also Salafi-influenced bunch in the '80s
who come up and start to give a political

01:02:10.010 --> 01:02:11.210
critique of the regime.

01:02:11.210 --> 01:02:15.850
And in the '90s, after the Gulf War, they
come into their own as very critical of the

01:02:15.850 --> 01:02:17.030
regime.

01:02:17.030 --> 01:02:23.940
After 9/11, all of these guys come back and
rally to the regime and become big supporters

01:02:23.940 --> 01:02:24.980
of the regime.

01:02:24.980 --> 01:02:25.980
Right?

01:02:25.980 --> 01:02:30.640
I would argue because they saw al-Saud rule
under pressure.

01:02:30.640 --> 01:02:31.640
Right?

01:02:31.640 --> 01:02:35.670
If there was going to be some international
reaction to 9/11 against Saudi rule, they

01:02:35.670 --> 01:02:39.650
were going to be with the regime, because
in the end, these Salafists cannot -- you

01:02:39.650 --> 01:02:45.240
know, the reason they're not bin Laden is
that none of these guys can imagine a Salafi

01:02:45.240 --> 01:02:50.900
state in Arabia that is not ruled by the al-Saud.

01:02:50.900 --> 01:02:59.850
And I think that that's why I don't think
of them as the biggest problem for the Saudis.

01:02:59.850 --> 01:03:03.810
On the succession thing, I'll demonstrate
my ignorance even more by talking more about

01:03:03.810 --> 01:03:04.930
this.

01:03:04.930 --> 01:03:06.040
(Laughter.)

01:03:06.040 --> 01:03:12.880
Look, Nayef has been the chief of police for
38 years, right?

01:03:12.880 --> 01:03:16.460
Thirty-three years, something in there.

01:03:16.460 --> 01:03:21.930
And when you're the chief of police for that
long, you develop a certain view of politics,

01:03:21.930 --> 01:03:22.990
I think.

01:03:22.990 --> 01:03:28.900
We're not going to see any political reform
the way we would understand political reform,

01:03:28.900 --> 01:03:32.660
in terms of voting, in terms of legislatures.

01:03:32.660 --> 01:03:35.890
I don't think we're going to see that under
Nayef.

01:03:35.890 --> 01:03:43.020
On the other hand, I'm very reluctant to say
that he's going to roll back the small but

01:03:43.020 --> 01:03:50.880
real social changes that Abdullah has allowed,
particularly on the issue of women.

01:03:50.880 --> 01:03:54.330
These guys don't like to repudiate their predecessors.

01:03:54.330 --> 01:04:01.250
I mean, they do have that sense of family
cohesion and we've got to stick together.

01:04:01.250 --> 01:04:05.181
You know, even after King Saud was bounced,
they named the airport and the university

01:04:05.181 --> 01:04:09.360
and all sorts of things -- Faisal named all
those things after him.

01:04:09.360 --> 01:04:10.810
Right?

01:04:10.810 --> 01:04:17.640
And so I would be reluctant to say that Nayef
is, you know, the opposite of Abdullah.

01:04:17.640 --> 01:04:21.150
But I don't think that Nayef is a political
reformer, but I don't think Abdullah is a

01:04:21.150 --> 01:04:23.060
political reformer.

01:04:23.060 --> 01:04:29.870
I do think that a long-term consequence of
the events of 2011 are that eventually Saudi

01:04:29.870 --> 01:04:33.230
Arabia is going to have an elected legislature.

01:04:33.230 --> 01:04:37.440
I think that the political trends regionally
are moving in that direction.

01:04:37.440 --> 01:04:43.190
I think that the political ideological trends
in Saudi Arabia are moving in that direction.

01:04:43.190 --> 01:04:48.830
I think that in the next generation, there
will be more acceptance of that notion.

01:04:48.830 --> 01:04:53.960
I think it will be -- and then it will be
problematic, just like the legislature in

01:04:53.960 --> 01:05:00.410
Kuwait is problematic, just like the legislature
in other of these monarchies can be problematic

01:05:00.410 --> 01:05:01.480
for the monarch.

01:05:01.480 --> 01:05:04.770
But I think -- and this is a change in my
view.

01:05:04.770 --> 01:05:10.780
Before 2011, I didn't think the Saudis would
go that way at all, but now I think they probably

01:05:10.780 --> 01:05:12.360
will, just not under Nayef.

01:05:12.360 --> 01:05:13.360
&gt;&gt;STARES: OK.

01:05:13.360 --> 01:05:14.360
Michele Dunne?

01:05:14.360 --> 01:05:15.720
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: Thank you.

01:05:15.720 --> 01:05:20.190
Elise actually asked my question about the
Salafis, but I just wanted to add a note on

01:05:20.190 --> 01:05:21.410
something else.

01:05:21.410 --> 01:05:27.191
Greg, you were mentioning that the failure
of the opposition, or the inability of the

01:05:27.191 --> 01:05:30.850
opposition in Saudi Arabia to unite being
one of the reasons why you weren't expecting

01:05:30.850 --> 01:05:33.530
-- or why you think it's stable.

01:05:33.530 --> 01:05:35.820
And that's empirically true, that they failed
to unite.

01:05:35.820 --> 01:05:42.650
But I just wanted to point out that in Tunisia
and Egypt, this happened very quickly.

01:05:42.650 --> 01:05:48.170
You know, all of a sudden we're -- you know,
these disparate opposition forces had tried

01:05:48.170 --> 01:05:52.650
and failed and they looked weak and they looked
feckless and, you know, all that kind of thing.

01:05:52.650 --> 01:05:57.980
And then, just, you know, overnight or over
the course of days and weeks, it happened.

01:05:57.980 --> 01:06:02.630
So it's not -- again, as Toby said, I think,
you know, the fact that it didn't happen in

01:06:02.630 --> 01:06:05.900
the past -- you know, it won't.

01:06:05.900 --> 01:06:08.000
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: You're right.

01:06:08.000 --> 01:06:13.670
And I'm one of those people who was very enamored
of the literature on the stability of Arab

01:06:13.670 --> 01:06:15.310
authoritarianism.

01:06:15.310 --> 01:06:20.550
And so I've had to do a rethink all this past
year about, you know, what we got wrong on

01:06:20.550 --> 01:06:21.550
that literature.

01:06:21.550 --> 01:06:24.760
I think that literature is still very valuable
for explaining why these regimes lasted as

01:06:24.760 --> 01:06:29.020
long as they did, but we certainly missed
things that were happening.

01:06:29.020 --> 01:06:33.900
And there are the hints of that in Saudi Arabia,
of people trying to cross ideological and

01:06:33.900 --> 01:06:36.140
sectarian lines to talk to each other.

01:06:36.140 --> 01:06:41.160
And, you know, occasionally even issuing,
you know, little proclamations.

01:06:41.160 --> 01:06:48.730
But to me, here -- to me, for Saudi Arabia
here was the moment that crystallized the

01:06:48.730 --> 01:06:58.030
fact that nothing was going to happen: As
Ben Ali fell -- right after Ben Ali fell and

01:06:58.030 --> 01:07:06.760
as Egyptians were starting the Tahrir Square
revolt, there was a big flood in Jeddah, killed

01:07:06.760 --> 01:07:09.210
10, 12 people.

01:07:09.210 --> 01:07:14.540
This is on top of a flood a couple years before,
which killed over 100 people, after which,

01:07:14.540 --> 01:07:20.680
the king himself said, this will not happen
again; we will make sure that this does not

01:07:20.680 --> 01:07:21.680
happen again.

01:07:21.680 --> 01:07:22.850
And then it happened again.

01:07:22.850 --> 01:07:31.730
I said, "Wow, if there's ever a time when
people should come into the streets in Jeddah,

01:07:31.730 --> 01:07:35.710
the ones that aren't flooded anyway, this
is it."

01:07:35.710 --> 01:07:41.160
Ben Ali, down -- the Egyptians, in the streets
and everyone in Saudi Arabia watching them.

01:07:41.160 --> 01:07:47.970
And here, the regime has failed in a promise
that should have been easy for them to keep,

01:07:47.970 --> 01:07:52.230
because it's just spending money getting the
engineering right and, you know, stopping

01:07:52.230 --> 01:07:53.230
this.

01:07:53.230 --> 01:07:54.710
And they didn't do it.

01:07:54.710 --> 01:07:57.130
Nothing happened.

01:07:57.130 --> 01:07:58.130
I was floored.

01:07:58.130 --> 01:07:59.130
I was flabbergasted.

01:07:59.130 --> 01:08:01.900
I thought this was a moment that something
might happen in Saudi Arabia.

01:08:01.900 --> 01:08:02.900
Nothing happened.

01:08:02.900 --> 01:08:05.740
&gt;&gt;JONES: Well, I think, you know, Egypt happened
spontaneously.

01:08:05.740 --> 01:08:06.840
I think that's right.

01:08:06.840 --> 01:08:12.290
But there was also -- there were forces on
this sort of -- it was a civil society in

01:08:12.290 --> 01:08:14.840
Egypt that could mobilize different sectors.

01:08:14.840 --> 01:08:18.019
Maybe they hadn't previously thought of one
another as kind of being like-minded, at least

01:08:18.019 --> 01:08:22.009
on the question of revolution or political
change, but nevertheless had the capacity

01:08:22.009 --> 01:08:23.009
to organize.

01:08:23.009 --> 01:08:28.279
Saudi Arabia doesn't historically have the
same kind of vibrant -- there's not the ability

01:08:28.279 --> 01:08:30.299
or the space for Saudis to come together.

01:08:30.299 --> 01:08:35.580
But I think we're seeing those institutions
and organizational efforts begin to materialize.

01:08:35.580 --> 01:08:41.350
And the Jeddah floods might be the impetus
in one place, but the prospects are that it's

01:08:41.350 --> 01:08:47.670
going to have to take some time before all
of this can mature or be realized in a meaningful

01:08:47.670 --> 01:08:48.670
way.

01:08:48.670 --> 01:08:52.830
And let's face it, the Saudis also learned,
like others in the region did, from the Egyptian

01:08:52.830 --> 01:08:57.520
example in particular, about how to -- at
least how to attempt to preempt it from happening

01:08:57.520 --> 01:08:58.520
quickly.

01:08:58.520 --> 01:08:59.599
I think the jury's still out.

01:08:59.599 --> 01:09:02.000
I don't think the Arab Spring's over, by any
means.

01:09:02.000 --> 01:09:04.940
You know, I'm not predicting that Saudi Arabia
in the next six months is going to have a

01:09:04.940 --> 01:09:08.179
revolutionary moment.

01:09:08.179 --> 01:09:10.739
But I wouldn't say it's impossible either.

01:09:10.739 --> 01:09:14.609
&gt;&gt;STARES: Doug and then Mark.

01:09:14.609 --> 01:09:19.210
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: With respect to a relationship
between Saudi Arabia and Iraq, how much of

01:09:19.210 --> 01:09:21.929
it is personal and how much political?

01:09:21.929 --> 01:09:25.940
Is there some type of policy that Iraq could
realistically do?

01:09:25.940 --> 01:09:29.109
I mean, Maliki's figured out he doesn't have
to share power with the Sunnis and he's not

01:09:29.109 --> 01:09:30.819
going to, not for a generation.

01:09:30.819 --> 01:09:31.819
I think that's pretty clear.

01:09:31.819 --> 01:09:36.239
Is there something else he could do, you know,
vis-a-vis reaching out to the Saudis or taking

01:09:36.239 --> 01:09:38.739
some firmer stand against Iran?

01:09:38.739 --> 01:09:42.560
And is that just -- with the current regime,
does that calculus change three, five years

01:09:42.560 --> 01:09:43.560
from now?

01:09:43.560 --> 01:09:48.119
&gt;&gt;JONES: I just had a piece come out at a
USIP edited volume, with Scott Lasensky and

01:09:48.119 --> 01:09:53.150
Phebe Marr and others, that argues that the
Saudis approach this in a couple of ways,

01:09:53.150 --> 01:09:54.750
the Iraq dilemma for them.

01:09:54.750 --> 01:09:58.361
One is certainly personal and it has to do
with the way Maliki talked about Iran and

01:09:58.361 --> 01:09:59.579
about Saudi power in the region.

01:09:59.579 --> 01:10:01.550
And certainly for Abdullah, I think that's
the case.

01:10:01.550 --> 01:10:05.860
But I think there are other -- there are at
least two other political calculations, one

01:10:05.860 --> 01:10:11.210
big one, and that is that the Saudis are uncertain
about whether the U.S. is going to abandon

01:10:11.210 --> 01:10:12.210
them in the region.

01:10:12.210 --> 01:10:16.070
And the Saudis, as late as last year, believed
that the U.S. would maintain some kind of

01:10:16.070 --> 01:10:18.920
political-military commitment to Iraq and
wanted it to do so.

01:10:18.920 --> 01:10:21.530
All right, they don't want the Americans to
leave Iraq.

01:10:21.530 --> 01:10:22.680
They don't want -- well, too late.

01:10:22.680 --> 01:10:23.840
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: They didn't want us to go in.

01:10:23.840 --> 01:10:24.840
They didn't want us to leave.

01:10:24.840 --> 01:10:30.000
&gt;&gt;JONES: Right, so part of this was also an
attempt to wedge the Americans to maintain

01:10:30.000 --> 01:10:35.860
sort of an ongoing commitment to Iraq, that
if you leave, this could become a conflagration

01:10:35.860 --> 01:10:36.860
point.

01:10:36.860 --> 01:10:41.449
And the other part of that is is that the
Saudis also, I think, calculated wrongly that

01:10:41.449 --> 01:10:45.590
if they were unwilling to work with the Americans
or think constructively about how to build

01:10:45.590 --> 01:10:49.630
a better relationship, they might have some
leverage over the U.S. on Palestine.

01:10:49.630 --> 01:10:54.369
And that's -- if the Arab Spring didn't obviate
that, then it was never going to happen anyway.

01:10:54.369 --> 01:10:55.369
Right?

01:10:55.369 --> 01:10:57.940
So I think that's also part of what the Saudis
were thinking.

01:10:57.940 --> 01:11:04.600
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: Yeah, I mean, I think that -- this
is a guess on my part, but Abdullah has been

01:11:04.600 --> 01:11:06.910
pretty flexible on all sorts of things.

01:11:06.910 --> 01:11:09.800
But on this one he seems to have really dug
in his heels.

01:11:09.800 --> 01:11:16.960
And I don't -- I just don't think that -- I
mean, any new Saudi initiative toward Iraq

01:11:16.960 --> 01:11:22.860
I think is going to depend on a change in
leadership at the top.

01:11:22.860 --> 01:11:31.090
I think there are people in Saudi who recognize
that their approach to Iraq is self-defeating,

01:11:31.090 --> 01:11:35.440
that it is, in effect, leaving the field open
to Iran.

01:11:35.440 --> 01:11:43.090
And I think that that's an argument that is
not publicly articulated in Saudi Arabia,

01:11:43.090 --> 01:11:53.099
but it is articulated at high enough levels
that perhaps there might be a rethink after

01:11:53.099 --> 01:11:54.940
the current king goes.

01:11:54.940 --> 01:11:57.280
&gt;&gt;STARES: Mark Finley?

01:11:57.280 --> 01:11:59.619
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: Thank you.

01:11:59.619 --> 01:12:06.051
The sentiment expressed throughout the conversation,
by both the speakers and the guests, about

01:12:06.051 --> 01:12:10.300
-- you know, for a variety of demographic
and social reasons, you know, whatever the

01:12:10.300 --> 01:12:15.920
status quo is, you know, the logic is inexorable
that, you know, the price of oil needed to

01:12:15.920 --> 01:12:18.360
sustain it goes higher.

01:12:18.360 --> 01:12:22.389
Obviously the price of oil can't go higher
forever, and so my question is -- at some

01:12:22.389 --> 01:12:25.210
point the regime has to deal with coping mechanisms.

01:12:25.210 --> 01:12:30.409
And here's my question: Among the array of
coping mechanisms that I can think of, cutting

01:12:30.409 --> 01:12:35.190
subsidies, pursuing -- somebody mentioned
nuclear power, other forms of energy, raising

01:12:35.190 --> 01:12:40.810
production capacity, inviting in foreign investment
-- which of these coping mechanisms is likely

01:12:40.810 --> 01:12:46.070
to be most -- least unattractive to the Saudi
body politic?

01:12:46.070 --> 01:12:47.180
&gt;&gt;JONES: Least --

01:12:47.180 --> 01:12:48.679
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: Least unattractive?

01:12:48.679 --> 01:12:49.679
Nuclear power.

01:12:49.679 --> 01:12:50.679
&gt;&gt;JONES: That's it.

01:12:50.679 --> 01:12:51.679
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: Build as many plants as you want.

01:12:51.679 --> 01:12:56.450
&gt;&gt;JONES: Yeah, I mean, I think it's the only
route that would basically -- it would not

01:12:56.450 --> 01:12:59.179
affect Saudi citizens in any meaningful way.

01:12:59.179 --> 01:13:01.500
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: Look, there are other things that
can be done.

01:13:01.500 --> 01:13:05.320
This is not -- see, this is where I think
Toby and I differ.

01:13:05.320 --> 01:13:07.949
I don't think this is a fragile regime.

01:13:07.949 --> 01:13:13.969
I think that this regime could in fact reduce
subsidies, especially now in a time of, you

01:13:13.969 --> 01:13:15.309
know, pretty good economic time.

01:13:15.309 --> 01:13:20.389
I mean, with all the unemployment issues and
all, this is not a bad economic time in Saudi

01:13:20.389 --> 01:13:22.659
Arabia.

01:13:22.659 --> 01:13:23.659
They could do it.

01:13:23.659 --> 01:13:25.510
They just -- they have to summon the political
will.

01:13:25.510 --> 01:13:32.710
And there are ways to do it that can lessen
its impact on those who would be most affected.

01:13:32.710 --> 01:13:34.570
All right?

01:13:34.570 --> 01:13:37.150
And then there are some technical fixes that
have to be done.

01:13:37.150 --> 01:13:38.150
Right?

01:13:38.150 --> 01:13:43.630
There are some -- these aren't sexy and they
aren't political, but you know, aging infrastructure

01:13:43.630 --> 01:13:47.580
uses more energy than new infrastructure.

01:13:47.580 --> 01:13:53.080
And, you know, you can get more natural gas
if you actually -- I mean, I know they drill

01:13:53.080 --> 01:13:58.269
dry holes and all, but you can presumably
get more natural gas in Saudi Arabia and stop

01:13:58.269 --> 01:14:03.940
burning so much oil to generate electricity.

01:14:03.940 --> 01:14:07.329
But no, the easiest thing is just build all
those nuclear power plants.

01:14:07.329 --> 01:14:09.110
But they're slow off the dime on that.

01:14:09.110 --> 01:14:14.050
I think that they can deal with the subsidy
issue and it won't shake the regime.

01:14:14.050 --> 01:14:15.830
But this would be a good time to do it, frankly.

01:14:15.830 --> 01:14:19.480
&gt;&gt;JONES: Well, but the only time they can
sort of end the sort of -- what is really

01:14:19.480 --> 01:14:24.769
a systematic and structural sweeping subsidy
regime that wouldn't affect people is to dip

01:14:24.769 --> 01:14:25.769
into their savings.

01:14:25.769 --> 01:14:26.769
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: They've got plenty right now.

01:14:26.769 --> 01:14:29.630
&gt;&gt;JONES: And they've got plenty right now,
but you said yourself that there's, you know,

01:14:29.630 --> 01:14:33.280
the oil market may not bear $110 a barrel
oil for the foreseeable future.

01:14:33.280 --> 01:14:36.290
I mean, that brings into play alternative
energies, whether it's shale oil or whether

01:14:36.290 --> 01:14:40.940
it's natural gas or whether it's other things,
that may make the Saudis no longer competitive.

01:14:40.940 --> 01:14:44.360
It may render irrelevant the question of spare
capacity.

01:14:44.360 --> 01:14:46.170
And then the Saudis really lose their ability
to manage.

01:14:46.170 --> 01:14:48.980
So if they think they can end subsidies, let
them try.

01:14:48.980 --> 01:14:52.599
I think it unleashes all kinds of things that
would be problematic for them at home, as

01:14:52.599 --> 01:14:54.820
well as their place in the global economy.

01:14:54.820 --> 01:14:58.900
In fact, I think we should advocate for that
and then see what happens.

01:14:58.900 --> 01:14:59.900
(Laughter.)

01:14:59.900 --> 01:15:03.309
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: You and I can get behind this policy,
but for different reasons.

01:15:03.309 --> 01:15:04.989
&gt;&gt;STARES: (Off mic.)

01:15:04.989 --> 01:15:06.679
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: Thank you.

01:15:06.679 --> 01:15:13.380
Thank you both for your presentation and given
the Saudis' interest in the status quo, and

01:15:13.380 --> 01:15:18.579
certainly stability in the region, it was
easy to understand their concern with the

01:15:18.579 --> 01:15:26.520
Mubarak fall, maybe also with Qadhafi, given
kind of the peripheral of Libya and Saudi

01:15:26.520 --> 01:15:27.520
Arabia.

01:15:27.520 --> 01:15:31.760
I'd be interested in hearing more about how
you think they are looking at Syria.

01:15:31.760 --> 01:15:37.489
Bashar al-Assad may not be a very well-loved
entity in Riyadh, but still, the Syria question

01:15:37.489 --> 01:15:38.489
--

01:15:38.489 --> 01:15:39.489
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: That's an understatement.

01:15:39.489 --> 01:15:41.180
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: -- is a lot more complex in
terms of what could happen.

01:15:41.180 --> 01:15:44.940
So they're watching the (off mic) in Egypt
or al-Nahda, in Tunisia.

01:15:44.940 --> 01:15:47.719
But what could happen in Syria would be very
different.

01:15:47.719 --> 01:15:51.239
And the other thing I was interested in to
hear your comments on, the dynamics between

01:15:51.239 --> 01:15:57.400
Saudi Arabia and Qatar, given the rising role
of Doha in dealing with issues like Syria

01:15:57.400 --> 01:15:58.881
and Libya and others.

01:15:58.881 --> 01:16:04.130
And how are those two playing, as they see
their role in regional politics?

01:16:04.130 --> 01:16:10.400
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: Well, okay, I'll take the first swing,
Ambassador.

01:16:10.400 --> 01:16:16.679
Syria: I think that if the Saudi media is
any indication of official thinking, and I

01:16:16.679 --> 01:16:22.530
think given the uniformity in which -- with
which I see the Saudi media dealing with Syria,

01:16:22.530 --> 01:16:28.440
I think it probably is -- they've washed their
hands of Bashar al-Assad.

01:16:28.440 --> 01:16:34.281
I mean, the worst things are said about him
every day, even in the most mainstream, you

01:16:34.281 --> 01:16:39.789
know, kind of -- the most responsible Saudi
media, the worst things are said about Bashar

01:16:39.789 --> 01:16:40.789
al-Assad.

01:16:40.789 --> 01:16:48.030
And it seems to me that, despite the uncertainties
of his fall, the elites have basically come

01:16:48.030 --> 01:16:52.030
to the conclusion that they're better off
without him than with him.

01:16:52.030 --> 01:16:59.610
And I think that this is largely driven by
their view of their regional competition with

01:16:59.610 --> 01:17:01.440
Iran.

01:17:01.440 --> 01:17:03.570
Will there be problems in reconstructing?

01:17:03.570 --> 01:17:04.570
Obviously.

01:17:04.570 --> 01:17:08.580
I think the Saudis feel that they have levers
that they can exercise.

01:17:08.580 --> 01:17:11.139
&gt;&gt;JONES: But if they have a plan --

01:17:11.139 --> 01:17:12.480
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: We don't see it.

01:17:12.480 --> 01:17:16.000
&gt;&gt;JONES: -- for a post-Assad Syria, what's
it look like?

01:17:16.000 --> 01:17:17.000
Who takes power?

01:17:17.000 --> 01:17:18.000
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: Who does have a plan?

01:17:18.000 --> 01:17:19.000
&gt;&gt;JONES: And if --

01:17:19.000 --> 01:17:21.070
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: Nobody has a plan for -- not even
the Syrian opposition has a plan for --

01:17:21.070 --> 01:17:22.070
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: Not even the opposition.

01:17:22.070 --> 01:17:23.070
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: -- has a plan for a post-Assad Syria.

01:17:23.070 --> 01:17:26.449
&gt;&gt;JONES: Right, so I mean, what is the end
game in Syria for the Saudis and the --

01:17:26.449 --> 01:17:32.860
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: The end game is to get the damn Shi'ites
out and cut the Iranians down to size, right?

01:17:32.860 --> 01:17:37.700
&gt;&gt;JONES: So it's just to replace Assad with
a group of elites that are more favorable

01:17:37.700 --> 01:17:38.700
to the Saudis.

01:17:38.700 --> 01:17:39.700
At least that seems --

01:17:39.700 --> 01:17:40.700
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: And are more Sunni.

01:17:40.700 --> 01:17:41.700
&gt;&gt;JONES: -- and you figure out who that is,
I suppose.

01:17:41.700 --> 01:17:43.780
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: I mean, that could be generated through
elections.

01:17:43.780 --> 01:17:45.340
That could be generated any number of ways.

01:17:45.340 --> 01:17:51.420
I think that the Saudis are realistic enough
to know that given what's happened this year,

01:17:51.420 --> 01:17:56.289
it would be very, very hard anywhere to have
a change of regime that doesn't have at least

01:17:56.289 --> 01:17:57.679
some kind of an election.

01:17:57.679 --> 01:18:02.650
Now, you know, they can try to manipulate
it and things like that, but, I mean, I don't

01:18:02.650 --> 01:18:06.559
think that they're powerful enough to prevent
that and I think that they realize that they've

01:18:06.559 --> 01:18:08.210
got to swim with this tide.

01:18:08.210 --> 01:18:11.320
Maybe the tide changes after a while, but
this is a tide they have to swim with.

01:18:11.320 --> 01:18:13.510
&gt;&gt;STARES: What is their pre-Assad plan?

01:18:13.510 --> 01:18:16.630
That's what I'm more interested in, rather
than the post one?

01:18:16.630 --> 01:18:19.610
We jumped over a major event here.

01:18:19.610 --> 01:18:20.909
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: Oh, you mean the fall of the regime?

01:18:20.909 --> 01:18:22.139
&gt;&gt;STARES: What do they do now?

01:18:22.139 --> 01:18:23.139
Yeah, what do --

01:18:23.139 --> 01:18:24.801
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: I don't think anybody has any bright
ideas.

01:18:24.801 --> 01:18:27.389
I think they do more of the same.

01:18:27.389 --> 01:18:30.880
They're going to ask us, "What are you going
to do about this?"

01:18:30.880 --> 01:18:37.980
&gt;&gt;JONES: There is some symbolic capital that's
earned by supporting, right, the fall of a

01:18:37.980 --> 01:18:42.010
quasi-unpopular -- I don't know, is Assad
unpopular at home?

01:18:42.010 --> 01:18:44.139
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: Seems to be unpopular in certain
circles.

01:18:44.139 --> 01:18:49.230
&gt;&gt;JONES: Syria is certainly divided, I think,
on the viability of Assad going forward, right?

01:18:49.230 --> 01:18:53.909
But the Saudis also, I think, generate some
symbolic capital outside of Syria by saying

01:18:53.909 --> 01:18:58.230
that they support and that they're not a counterrevolutionary
force, especially at home.

01:18:58.230 --> 01:19:01.820
There's also -- I mean, the most cynical side
of me says that the Saudis actually benefit

01:19:01.820 --> 01:19:04.980
a great deal by making sure that if there's
going to be crisis in the region that it happens

01:19:04.980 --> 01:19:06.489
somewhere else.

01:19:06.489 --> 01:19:12.270
If they're seen to be involved and attempting
to manage things, then that maybe pays dividends.

01:19:12.270 --> 01:19:17.949
But if you're talking about the Saudis being
ineffective, I mean, this would be a terrible

01:19:17.949 --> 01:19:20.000
way to approach foreign policy, right?

01:19:20.000 --> 01:19:24.480
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: Do you think the Saudis are powerful
enough that they can cause uprisings in Syria?

01:19:24.480 --> 01:19:25.579
&gt;&gt;JONES: No.

01:19:25.579 --> 01:19:28.050
But there were already uprisings in Syria.

01:19:28.050 --> 01:19:29.050
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: Yeah.

01:19:29.050 --> 01:19:33.090
&gt;&gt;JONES: So you throw the weight of the Arab
League behind it and for Syrian opposition

01:19:33.090 --> 01:19:37.950
forces, that's a pretty powerful indication
of what's possible, if you sustain the fight.

01:19:37.950 --> 01:19:41.400
Now ultimately, the Arab League has made,
you know, very clear gestures that the next

01:19:41.400 --> 01:19:43.389
stop is the U.N. Security Council.

01:19:43.389 --> 01:19:49.110
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: Look, I think that it's useful to
talk about how the Saudis approach Syria,

01:19:49.110 --> 01:19:52.630
but it's not nearly as important as how some
other countries approach Syria.

01:19:52.630 --> 01:19:58.260
And Turkey is a lot more important than Saudi
Arabia on the immediate question of where

01:19:58.260 --> 01:20:02.389
this uprising goes and where this regime goes.

01:20:02.389 --> 01:20:06.660
&gt;&gt;JONES: I think the question of Qatar's role
has gotten a lot of attention.

01:20:06.660 --> 01:20:07.660
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: Too much.

01:20:07.660 --> 01:20:12.800
&gt;&gt;JONES: And perhaps there is -- you know,
historically there's been a rivalry.

01:20:12.800 --> 01:20:18.120
But it's interesting that Qatar hasn't been
particularly assertive -- (inaudible) -- right,

01:20:18.120 --> 01:20:19.940
close to the Saudi hegemonic heartland.

01:20:19.940 --> 01:20:20.940
You don't see a --

01:20:20.940 --> 01:20:21.940
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: Absolutely silent on --

01:20:21.940 --> 01:20:26.100
&gt;&gt;JONES: Qatar's not pushing on issues that
the Saudis really think are a point of emphasis,

01:20:26.100 --> 01:20:27.199
and I don't think they would.

01:20:27.199 --> 01:20:31.449
I think the Saudis are content to let them
-- in fact, I don't think Saudi has all that

01:20:31.449 --> 01:20:34.820
-- or historically has had all that much influence
in Yemen.

01:20:34.820 --> 01:20:38.650
And if the Saudis believe that the Qataris
could come in and negotiate some kind of compromised

01:20:38.650 --> 01:20:41.039
transition of power, more -- the Saudis can't
do it.

01:20:41.039 --> 01:20:42.550
You know, but I don't --

01:20:42.550 --> 01:20:43.969
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: I disagree about Yemen.

01:20:43.969 --> 01:20:44.969
&gt;&gt;STARES: Yeah, I do, too.

01:20:44.969 --> 01:20:47.719
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: I think the Saudis have had lots
of power in Yemen.

01:20:47.719 --> 01:20:50.179
But I wrote a book about that, so I have a
stake in it.

01:20:50.179 --> 01:20:51.179
(Laughter.)

01:20:51.179 --> 01:21:01.130
Look, Qatari foreign policy is a vanity project
by a couple of guys who are -- who want to

01:21:01.130 --> 01:21:03.329
play a big role in the world.

01:21:03.329 --> 01:21:06.260
And thus, I find it very difficult to analyze
it.

01:21:06.260 --> 01:21:07.260
(Laughter.)

01:21:07.260 --> 01:21:11.369
I mean, it's not based upon some kind of state
interest.

01:21:11.369 --> 01:21:12.369
Right?

01:21:12.369 --> 01:21:14.719
It's based on a splash.

01:21:14.719 --> 01:21:16.070
And more power to them.

01:21:16.070 --> 01:21:20.199
And many of the things they've done, including
Al-Jazeera, have been extremely positive for

01:21:20.199 --> 01:21:22.409
the region.

01:21:22.409 --> 01:21:29.690
But it's an irritant sometimes to the Saudis,
but I don't think it's anything more than

01:21:29.690 --> 01:21:31.139
an irritant.

01:21:31.139 --> 01:21:34.710
I think we all spend too much time talking
about it.

01:21:34.710 --> 01:21:37.139
I don't think it's that important.

01:21:37.139 --> 01:21:39.000
&gt;&gt;STARES: OK.

01:21:39.000 --> 01:21:42.170
We're coming to the end.

01:21:42.170 --> 01:21:43.969
(Arnold ?), you have the last question.

01:21:43.969 --> 01:21:46.710
&gt;&gt;QUESTIONER: Yeah, I wonder if you could
quickly address the problem of succession

01:21:46.710 --> 01:21:47.710
after Prince Nayef.

01:21:47.710 --> 01:21:53.940
How many of the Sudairi Seven are left, and
does that line still hold?

01:21:53.940 --> 01:22:00.120
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: Yeah, Prince Salman, who is now defense
minister, long-time governor of Riyadh, one

01:22:00.120 --> 01:22:01.519
of the full brothers.

01:22:01.519 --> 01:22:10.340
You know, if I were a betting man, I would
put him next.

01:22:10.340 --> 01:22:12.670
There's -- Simon, help me.

01:22:12.670 --> 01:22:14.900
Prince Ahmed at interior --

01:22:14.900 --> 01:22:15.900
&gt;&gt;MR.

01:22:15.900 --> 01:22:16.900
: Abdul Rahman.

01:22:16.900 --> 01:22:20.670
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: There's Abdul Rahman at interior,
right?

01:22:20.670 --> 01:22:22.590
Deputy minister of interior?

01:22:22.590 --> 01:22:23.590
&gt;&gt;MR.

01:22:23.590 --> 01:22:24.590
: (Off mic.)

01:22:24.590 --> 01:22:28.039
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: Yeah, Ahmad at interior, right?

01:22:28.039 --> 01:22:32.460
Because he's the only one, other than Salman,
who has a government job, right?

01:22:32.460 --> 01:22:33.460
&gt;&gt;MR.

01:22:33.460 --> 01:22:34.460
: Yeah.

01:22:34.460 --> 01:22:38.130
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: I mean, I think it's interesting
that Salman has moved to the defense ministry,

01:22:38.130 --> 01:22:44.659
you know, because now he can say, having been
governor of Riyadh, which is one of the most

01:22:44.659 --> 01:22:48.989
important positions in the kingdom, for a
long, long, long, long time, he's now a member

01:22:48.989 --> 01:22:49.989
of the Cabinet.

01:22:49.989 --> 01:22:52.800
He's dealing with foreign policy.

01:22:52.800 --> 01:22:57.090
In many ways, one could see this almost as
a grooming.

01:22:57.090 --> 01:23:10.650
But the internal dynamics of this are opaque
to me and the actuarial tables are -- do not

01:23:10.650 --> 01:23:15.650
give us good indication as to how this line
will go.

01:23:15.650 --> 01:23:18.769
&gt;&gt;JONES: I think there's two -- and I think
this is cross-generational.

01:23:18.769 --> 01:23:24.760
I think there's two things that are fundamentally
true about the royal family.

01:23:24.760 --> 01:23:26.760
One is that they're not ideological.

01:23:26.760 --> 01:23:28.239
They're pragmatists.

01:23:28.239 --> 01:23:29.239
Right?

01:23:29.239 --> 01:23:34.039
And the second is that they are primarily,
overwhelmingly, in total agreement, concerned

01:23:34.039 --> 01:23:35.039
with self-preservation.

01:23:35.039 --> 01:23:37.719
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: Those are two points or one?

01:23:37.719 --> 01:23:38.719
(Laughter.)

01:23:38.719 --> 01:23:39.719
&gt;&gt;JONES: Those are two.

01:23:39.719 --> 01:23:40.719
&gt;&gt;GAUSE: Oh, OK.

01:23:40.719 --> 01:23:41.719
&gt;&gt;JONES: Right.

01:23:41.719 --> 01:23:42.719
So they're not ideological, although they'll
play ideology.

01:23:42.719 --> 01:23:43.719
Right?

01:23:43.719 --> 01:23:44.989
And they'll use it for their purposes.

01:23:44.989 --> 01:23:47.540
And they're interested in preserving their
power and their privilege.

01:23:47.540 --> 01:23:52.719
And, you know, that's incentive for people
to sort of stick together.

01:23:52.719 --> 01:23:54.700
&gt;&gt;STARES: Okay.

01:23:54.700 --> 01:23:57.949
Well, this has been a fascinating discussion.

01:23:57.949 --> 01:24:00.460
I've certainly learned a lot from it.

01:24:00.460 --> 01:24:07.630
I want to thank you all for coming here today.

01:24:07.630 --> 01:24:13.170
(Applause.)

01:24:13.170 --> 01:24:22.389
And please thank our (president ?).

